gb-006-title = the door to the goatnet did not open
gb-006-what = the goatnet's management host was not reachable over the mesh, so the SSM door was tried, and it did not open.
gb-006-cause-1 = no AWS credentials in the keychain for aws-vault (the profile in deploy.aws_profile)
gb-006-cause-2 = the SSM agent on the management host is not connected (TargetNotConnected)
gb-006-cause-3 = the door registry row is stale
gb-007-title = a secret the play needs is not in your environment
gb-007-what = the play templates one or more variables from the environment and they are not set, so the run was refused before any host was touched.
gb-007-cause-1 = the refs file for this component was never written
gb-007-cause-2 = the command was run without `op run`
gb-008-title = a mutation needs --yes when there is no terminal
gb-008-what = deploy, release, rollback, fastlane, standup, teardown, join-node, federate, apply and grow change state; without a terminal to confirm on, they refuse unless --yes is passed (contract C10). grow asks only when stdin and stderr are both a terminal, so with stderr sent to a file it needs --yes too.
gb-008-cause-1 = run from a script, CI or a pipe
gb-009-title = not a current main checkout
gb-009-what = releases and deploys run from the invoking checkout, and only a main checkout kept current with origin carries the pin that was merged. A branch or detached checkout can deploy a stale pin and verify green.
gb-009-cause-1 = running from a feature-branch worktree
gb-009-cause-2 = running from a detached HEAD
gb-009-cause-3 = main is behind origin/main
gb-010-title = a Python dependency is missing
gb-010-what = the pins and intents render through PyYAML and jsonschema, and python3 cannot import one of them.
gb-010-cause-1 = a fresh machine
gb-010-cause-2 = a venv that is not on PATH
gb-011-title = only a repository admin may nominate a release candidate
gb-011-what = the GitHub login in use does not hold the admin role on the repository; the rc-validate workflow deletes a tag pushed by anyone else (ADR 1173).
gb-011-cause-1 = gh is logged in as a different account
gb-011-cause-2 = the account is a maintainer, not an admin
gb-077-title = this checkout's origin is not where release candidates are cut
gb-077-what = the repository that origin names does not run rc-validate.yml, so a tag pushed there is never validated or built where the ladder reads images; the run refuses before the tag (ADR 1173).
gb-077-cause-1 = the checkout is a clone of a mirror or a fork, not the primary repository
gb-078-title = no release signer is known, so the update refuses
gb-078-what = `goatbuilder update` checks SHA256SUMS against the repository whose release workflow signed it. It found none: the working directory is in no checkout whose ops/goatbuilder/goatbuilder.yaml names repo.release_source or repo.source, and this binary was built outside a release workflow, so it names no publisher itself. Nothing was installed.
gb-078-cause-1 = goatbuilder update ran outside a deployment checkout with a locally built binary
gb-386-title = the served release is older, so --force does not install it
gb-386-what = `goatbuilder update --force` reinstalls the version this binary already is. The surface serves a version that is older than this binary, or one that does not compare as a version, so installing it would go back. That takes its own flag, `--allow-downgrade`. Nothing was downloaded or installed.
gb-386-cause-1 = the goatnet's get surface still serves an older release than the one installed here
gb-386-cause-2 = the binary was installed from a newer release or built from source
gb-430-title = this machine class declares no NetBird groups of its own, so join-node does not enrol it
gb-430-what = `join-node` mints a node's setup key with the groups the founder's own peer holds, which are the site's INFRA group: what a management host joins, a core node or a phone node (which converges as core). No other class may take them: a break-glass node reaches only INFRA and NETWORK on port 22 (ADR 1114), a network-jump host is kept out of INFRA (ADR 1131), and no record puts an observability host there. Until the class declares the groups its node joins, the run is refused at phase 1, before the node changes, and phase 10 refuses on its own. Nothing was changed.
gb-430-cause-1 = join-node --class <class> for a class other than core or phone, over a range that includes phase 10
gb-265-title = the run is waiting on the other driver
gb-265-what = The peering is declared with two drivers (`drivers: two`): an edge driver that reaches only the edge host and a controller that reaches the core. This phase needs the edge, so the controller stopped before acting and handed it to the edge driver. The stop prints the exact command to run there, the file it writes, and the two commands to run here after it; with -o json the report carries them as the member handoff. It stops as a by-hand stop does (exit 0).
gb-265-cause-1 = the peering's phase needs the edge host, and this machine is the controller
gb-266-title = the controller's checkout is not clean, so no hand-off is given
gb-266-what = A two-driver peering's hand-off pins the edge driver's checkout to the controller's commit, so the controller gives one only when its own checkout is a git checkout with nothing uncommitted. The run stopped at the phase that needs the edge, before acting, and names each file with changes.
gb-266-cause-1 = phase 10 placed rendered files, or admission wrote peerings.yml, and they are not committed yet
gb-266-cause-2 = a file was edited by hand and not committed
gb-266-cause-3 = the deployment's directory is not a git checkout
gb-269-title = a secret for the node still holds a secret store's reference
gb-269-what = A value join-node hands to a node, such as the first-login password phase 2 seeds the SSH key with, still begins with a secret store's reference (op://, bao://, bw:// or the declared ref_prefix) after the secrets injector ran. Sent as it is, the node would take the reference as the secret. Nothing was sent to the node. The message names the variable and the scheme, never the reference.
gb-269-cause-1 = the store's tool is not signed in, so with-secrets could not resolve the reference
gb-269-cause-2 = the variable was set to a reference under a store that resolves none (env, env-file)
gb-267-title = this part of a two-driver peering is not built yet
gb-267-what = A peering declared with two drivers (`drivers: two`) is handed off between the edge driver and the controller only at phases 1, 2 and 3, where the hand-off is built. These cases are refused before anything runs: the edge driver's own run (`goatbuilder federate <partner> --side edge`) of a phase other than 1 or 3; the controller's `goatbuilder federate import` of a phase other than 1 or 3; a two-driver run that would reach a phase after 3 that needs the edge (11, 12, 13 or 14), whose hand-off is designed and not built; and a two-driver run from phase 3 with FED_MEASURE=here, which measures one machine and so has no halves to hand off. That includes every `goatbuilder federate deploy`, which runs phase 10 then 11: it is refused before its plan is approved or rechecked, so phase 10 never admits the edge on our core.
gb-267-cause-1 = goatbuilder federate <partner> --side edge was run on the edge driver for a phase other than 1 or 3
gb-267-cause-2 = goatbuilder federate import <partner> <phase> <result> --sha256 <code> was run for a phase other than 1 or 3
gb-267-cause-3 = a two-driver run that would reach phase 11, 12, 13 or 14, such as --from 4 with no --to, or goatbuilder federate deploy (a run from 4 to 10 is not refused)
gb-267-cause-4 = a two-driver run from phase 3 with FED_MEASURE=here
gb-271-title = the peering's WireGuard interface does not hold the agreed peer and address
gb-271-what = Phase 11 writes the agreement's link values (the counterparty's public key, its allowed address and our link address) to the link's environment file, restarts goat-peering-wg@<interface>.service and reads the interface back with `wg show <interface> allowed-ips` and `ip -4 addr show dev <interface>`. The read-back did not find them, so the link the agreement describes is not up on this edge. The end of the message lists what the interface holds: each peer's public key and allowed addresses, then its addresses. No peer and no address is the interface as it is before phase 11.
gb-271-cause-1 = the link unit is still running with the environment it had before phase 11: the agreement's peer and address were written to the link's environment file, but goat-peering-wg@<interface>.service was not restarted to read them (a phase 11 cut off between writing the file and the restart, or run by a role from before the restart-on-read check)
gb-271-cause-2 = the unit was restarted and goat-peering-wg-link up stopped partway: its journal says where
gb-271-cause-3 = something other than this role changed the interface after the unit set it up (another peering's unit, or wg set by hand)
gb-272-title = the partner's acceptance result does not verify
gb-272-what = In a bilateral acceptance session each side signs its phase 14 result and the other verifies it under the key bound to that side at phase 5, the same key that signs its federation package. The signature document beside the partner's result did not verify: it is malformed, it names another key, the result read is not the one it signs, or the signature does not verify under the bound key. Nothing from it is combined (docs/design/federation-acceptance.md §6.1).
gb-272-cause-1 = the result or its signature document was altered, or the result was read before the partner finished writing it
gb-272-cause-2 = the partner signed with a key other than the one bound to them at phase 5 (their federation key was rotated, or the wrong key file was used)
gb-272-cause-3 = the signature document is not `goat-acceptance-signature/v1`, or a member is missing or malformed
gb-273-title = the partner's acceptance result is not from this session
gb-273-what = The partner's signed acceptance result is for another agreement, session or attempt, is signed by or for another party, or does not echo the challenge issued to us, so it cannot be from this session's samples. It is refused before its signature is checked, and nothing from it is combined (docs/design/federation-acceptance.md §6.1).
gb-273-cause-1 = an earlier session's or attempt's result was read again (a replay, or a result left from before)
gb-273-cause-2 = the two sides hold different agreements: the digests read aloud at phase 6 differ
gb-273-cause-3 = our own result was read back as if it were the partner's
gb-274-title = the two sides derived different acceptance sessions
gb-274-what = Both sides of a peering work out the same acceptance session from the agreement each holds and an attempt number, with no file between them (docs/design/federation-acceptance.md §6.2). The partner's session is not the same as ours: the refusal names the members that differ. Nothing is combined with a partner on another session.
gb-274-cause-1 = the two sides hold different agreements: the digests read aloud at phase 6 differ, or one side composed again since
gb-274-cause-2 = the two sides are on different attempts
gb-274-cause-3 = a session file was edited, so its digest no longer covers its members
gb-275-title = phase 12 would deploy an edge that has none of the files it mounts
gb-275-what = Phase 8 mints the peering leaves, and places our CA beside them, in <work>/pki on the machine goatbuilder federate runs on. The edge router mounts each of those files by path on the edge host. The edge group's variables point the edge at files in <work>/pki, the inventory names the edge as another machine, and goat_zenoh_edge_install_certs is off, so nothing copies them there: the edge would start with nothing to mount and never reach the core. Phase 12 is refused before it starts.
gb-275-cause-1 = goatbuilder federate runs on an operator's workspace, not on the edge host, and the edge group's install of the peering certificates is off
gb-275-cause-2 = the inventory names this machine by an address of its own, not as ansible_connection=local
gb-285-title = the directory binds our previous signing key, and the seed that hands it over is missing
gb-285-what = Phase 5's directory step publishes our pointer signed by the key that signed our package. The directory binds our sovereign_id to the first key that wrote it, and takes a new key only through the entry, when the request is signed by the key it replaces. The directory binds another key than ours: our signing key was rotated, and FED_PREVIOUS_SIGNING_KEY is not set, is not readable or holds the seed of a third key, or the pointer names another of our peerings' packages. Nothing was published; the directory still serves what it held. The step is advisory, so phase 5 goes on.
gb-285-cause-1 = our signing key was rotated (FED_SIGNING_KEY names the new seed) and FED_PREVIOUS_SIGNING_KEY was not set
gb-285-cause-2 = FED_PREVIOUS_SIGNING_KEY names a seed other than the one the directory binds (the message names both keys)
gb-285-cause-3 = with one package per counterparty, the pointer names another peering's package, and the key is handed over only through the pointer
gb-261-title = the release's signature does not verify, so the update refuses
gb-261-what = `goatbuilder update` verifies the keyless signature over the release's SHA256SUMS (SHA256SUMS.cosign-bundle) itself, offline, against the Sigstore trusted root: the certificate chains to a Fulcio CA, Rekor's signed entry timestamp verifies, the entry names this file, and the certificate is the release signer's goatbuilder-release.yml at the served version's goatbuilder/v<version> tag. One of these failed, or the surface serves no signature. The message names the check. Nothing was installed.
gb-261-cause-1 = the surface serves a SHA256SUMS or a binary that the release workflow did not sign
gb-261-cause-2 = the signature is another repository's, another workflow's, or another version's
gb-261-cause-3 = Sigstore rotated its Fulcio CA or Rekor key after this goatbuilder was built
gb-261-cause-4 = the surface serves no SHA256SUMS.cosign-bundle
gb-262-title = the verb has no such phase
gb-262-what = `goatbuilder explain <verb> <phase>` answers from the phase catalog (ops/phases/catalog.yaml), by the phase's number or its id. The verb has no phase by that number or id. The message names the phases it has.
gb-262-cause-1 = a phase number outside the verb's range, or a mistyped id
gb-012-title = the candidate's image build did not succeed
gb-012-what = after the tag was pushed, the image workflow did not appear, or ran and failed, so there is no image to validate.
gb-012-cause-1 = the workflow is not triggered by this tag shape
gb-012-cause-2 = a build failure on the tagged commit
gb-013-title = the image is not in the registry
gb-013-what = the tag's image was not found under repo.image_namespace, so no digest can be pinned.
gb-013-cause-1 = crane is missing or unauthenticated — rc.sh resolves the digest with crane and discards its error, so a missing crane reads as the image not existing
gb-013-cause-2 = the build published under another name
gb-013-cause-3 = the package is private and the token cannot read it
gb-014-title = a live validation row failed
gb-014-what = the candidate deployed and a live row on the goatnet failed. On dev the candidate is dead; on preprod after a dev pass it stays open for investigation.
gb-014-cause-1 = a row exited 2 — it was never run: its toolchain is missing on this laptop (Playwright for the intake rows, a goat context for the invite rows); the candidate is untested, not dead
gb-014-cause-2 = a real regression in the candidate
gb-014-cause-3 = the row's runner settings are stale for this goatnet
gb-015-title = the checkout could not fast-forward after the pin landed
gb-015-what = the pin PR merged, and the local main has diverged from origin/main, so the deploy could not proceed from a current tree. The PR is in; re-running resumes.
gb-015-cause-1 = local commits on main
gb-016-title = no runner settings for the goatnet
gb-016-what = ops/validation/goatnet-runners.yaml has no entry for the goatnet the live rows should run on.
gb-016-cause-1 = a new goatnet without its runner row
gb-017-title = not converged on every host
gb-017-what = the play ran, and at least one host's ledger does not name the declared digest with action=deploy. Done is read from the ledger, never assumed.
gb-017-cause-1 = the play failed part-way on that host
gb-017-cause-2 = the host is single-homed on another router role and was skipped by design (ADR 1084)
gb-017-cause-3 = the host was unreachable
gb-018-title = a required flag is missing or wrong
gb-018-what = release needs --ack and --version or --tag; --from takes tag|dev|rows|preprod|prod; --from prod needs --tag.
gb-018-cause-1 = the command was typed from memory
gb-019-title = the goatnet's CA key is not on this machine
gb-019-what = re-signing the intents needs <deploy.state_dir>/<goatnet>/ca.key.age and it is not there.
gb-019-cause-1 = this laptop never held that goatnet's CA key
gb-020-title = graduate.py did not produce the expected record
gb-020-what = graduation ran, and wrote no record, or the record names a different candidate_ref than the tag resolves to.
gb-020-cause-1 = an open graduation set blocking the move (ADR 1125)
gb-020-cause-2 = a candidate record edited by hand
gb-021-title = fastlane refused
gb-021-what = fastlane is the deliberate exception to the ladder and refuses an unpinned digest, an open graduation set, or an artifact whose rollback is not a pin revert.
gb-021-cause-1 = the ref has no digest under repo.image_namespace
gb-021-cause-2 = a graduation set is open for the component
gb-022-title = intent-sign failed
gb-022-what = re-signing a goatnet's intents after a pin move failed; the signed index is unchanged.
gb-022-cause-1 = the CA key passphrase was wrong or age could not decrypt it
gb-022-cause-2 = the enrollment Go module is not at ops/enrollment
gb-023-title = rollback refused or guided by artifact type
gb-023-what = the component's artifact type is data, cert, forward-only or reversible:false (refused, exit 3), or config/acl (guided, exit 4): its rollback is not a pin flip.
gb-023-cause-1 = the component carries a migration or a certificate
gb-024-title = the fixture is not a shape the mock converger knows
gb-024-what = on --mock, converger.yaml names a behaviour the scripted converger does not know, a record is unreadable, or nothing is declared for the environment.
gb-024-cause-1 = a hand-edited fixture
gb-025-title = the config does not validate
gb-025-what = ops/goatbuilder/goatbuilder.yaml failed the versioned schema or a cross-field rule. The config is generated; fix the source and regenerate.
gb-025-cause-1 = a hand edit to the generated file
gb-025-cause-2 = a pins or ladder change the generator was not run for
gb-026-title = status reads real hosts only through a converger
gb-026-what = without --mock, status has no converger to read hosts through yet.
gb-026-cause-1 = the push converger's status path is not built
gb-027-title = not inside a repository
gb-027-what = the repository root could not be resolved from the working directory and -C was not given.
gb-027-cause-1 = run outside a checkout
gb-028-title = a command the verb runs failed
gb-028-what = git, gh, crane, python or another tool exited non-zero; its own words follow and are interpreted below when the catalog knows them.
gb-028-cause-1 = see the tool's message
gb-029-title = the play failed on a group
gb-029-what = ansible-playbook exited non-zero for the group; the log path is in the row.
gb-029-cause-1 = a fatal task on a host (see the ansible entries below)
gb-029-cause-2 = a host unreachable over the chosen transport
gb-030-title = no host of the group is in the loaded inventories
gb-030-what = the group is declared in the pins but the inventories loaded for this goatnet carry no host for it, so nothing was deployed (declared, not built).
gb-030-cause-1 = a site declared ahead of its hardware
gb-030-cause-2 = the wrong inventory pair for this goatnet
gb-031-title = an op:// reference in your shell is incomplete
gb-031-what = `op run` validates every environment variable whose value starts with op://, not only the env-file, and refuses the whole run when one has fewer than vault/item/field.
gb-031-cause-1 = a variable exported from your shell profile for another project holds a two-part op:// reference
gb-032-title = the candidate record is open but the goatnet runs a different ref
gb-032-what = graduate.py's C6 check: a record for this component is still open while the goatnet now runs another ref — the record the preprod step superseded was never abandoned.
gb-032-cause-1 = a release run on a checkout older than #2039, which abandons the superseded record in the preprod change
gb-032-cause-2 = a candidate re-cut while the previous record stayed open
gb-033-title = promote refused a graduation step
gb-033-what = `goatbuilder promote` (the release ladder, ADR 1120/1125) refused: the record, the set, the ladder or the ref does not meet the rule the step names. Nothing graduates by itself; a refusal changes nothing.
gb-033-cause-1 = the component is not ready (soak, live rows, convergence evidence) — the lines under the refusal name each blocker
gb-033-cause-2 = the component is a member of an open graduation set and must graduate with it
gb-033-cause-3 = a ref that is not digest-pinned (…@sha256:…), or a hotfix without --ack
gb-033-cause-4 = no open record for the component, or the record named does not exist or already graduated
gb-034-title = the generated pins or intents differ from fleet-pins.yaml
gb-034-what = `goatbuilder pins --check` regenerated every member's pins.yml and every goatnet's intent from ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml and they differ from what is committed, or an intent is unsigned or tampered, or a governed pin is defined outside pins.yml.
gb-034-cause-1 = fleet-pins.yaml changed without regenerating
gb-034-cause-2 = a hand edit to a generated pins.yml or intent
gb-035-title = the goatnet's intent names no usable CA
gb-035-what = an intent has no ca_id, or one from which the CA's directory under <deploy.state_dir> cannot be derived, so it cannot be signed.
gb-035-cause-1 = a new goatnet whose intent was written without ca_id
gb-036-title = the component declares no image workflow
gb-036-what = release finds a candidate's image build by the component's image_workflow in fleet-pins.yaml, and there is none.
gb-036-cause-1 = a component added without image_workflow
gb-037-title = the ladder is missing a live rung
gb-037-what = ops/release/ladder.yaml has no live rung with a goatnet where the step needs one, or does not read.
gb-037-cause-1 = a rung marked not live while a goatnet is rebuilt
gb-037-cause-2 = a malformed ladder.yaml
gb-038-title = --from preprod-rows cannot resume this candidate
gb-038-what = resuming at the preprod rows needs preprod to run the candidate on main and exactly one open record for it; one of the two is missing.
gb-038-cause-1 = the preprod pin was never landed for this candidate
gb-038-cause-2 = the record was closed, or a second one opened
gb-039-title = a rollback on a fixture needs its environment
gb-039-what = on a fixture (--mock) a rollback declares the earlier ref in one environment and converges it there, so it needs --env; --list and --status read host ledgers, which a fixture does not have.
gb-039-cause-1 = --mock given without --env
gb-039-cause-2 = --list or --status given with --mock
gb-040-title = that directory is not a tutor workspace
gb-040-what = the tutor creates its workspace in an empty or new directory and deletes only a directory that carries its .goatbuilder-tutor marker; it refused to use or delete one that does not.
gb-040-cause-1 = --workspace (or $GOATBUILDER_TUTOR) points at a directory with other files in it
gb-041-title = there is no such lesson
gb-041-what = the tutor was asked for a lesson number it does not have.
gb-041-cause-1 = a number past the last lesson
gb-042-title = the live rows cannot run from this machine — the candidate is UNTESTED, not dead
gb-042-what = a release step reached the live acceptance rows, and this machine lacks what they need (the console's Playwright toolchain for a browser row, or a tool or context the runner reported with exit 2). The rows did not run; nothing about the candidate failed.
gb-042-cause-1 = a browser row is mapped and ops/goat-id-console/web/e2e/node_modules is missing, or node/npm is not installed
gb-042-cause-2 = the acceptance runner exited 2 (a tool or a goat context missing on this machine or on the host it runs goat on)
gb-043-title = the door to the rung did not open for its live rows
gb-043-what = the rows for a rung this laptop is not meshed to run goat on that rung's management host through its door (goat_via door:<env>), and the door did not open or printed no ssh target.
gb-043-cause-1 = aws-vault cannot reach the declared AWS profile (signed out, a malformed credential, or an expiring session)
gb-043-cause-2 = session-manager-plugin is not installed
gb-044-title = a release refuses before the tag because a secret is not set
gb-044-what = every rung this run will deploy to templates the component's secrets from your environment, and at least one is not set. Nothing was tagged, pinned or deployed.
gb-044-cause-1 = the run was not wrapped in op run with a .refs file naming those variables
gb-044-cause-2 = goat-credentials refs/goat.refs has no line for a variable (ops/ci/check-secrets-coverage.py names it)
gb-045-title = the published declarations are out of order or altered
gb-045-what = a declaration is applied only from an intact, ordered log (contract C2). A row whose signature does not match its content, or a serial not greater than one already accepted, is refused before anything is published, so a replayed or edited declaration never reaches a machine. A new serial at an earlier digest is accepted; that is a revert.
gb-045-cause-1 = a row of declared/publications.jsonl was edited by hand after it was published
gb-045-cause-2 = an older copy of the log was restored over a newer one, or a row was replayed
gb-046-title = the local declaration is not the source of record's
gb-046-what = a deploy applies only what the source of record declares (contract C3). A local copy behind it is fast-forwarded first, or refused under --frozen; a local copy ahead of it or diverged from it is always refused, because a declaration that is not in the source of record is never applied.
gb-046-cause-1 = the checkout or the fixture's declared/ was not fast-forwarded and --frozen was passed
gb-046-cause-2 = a declaration was edited locally instead of in the source of record
gb-047-title = a declaration must name who made it
gb-047-what = every declaration, and every record it converges, carries the person who made it (contract C4). A deploy, rollback or standup run without --ack is refused before anything is published.
gb-047-cause-1 = --ack was not passed, or was empty
gb-048-title = the goatnet is not proven built
gb-048-what = the acceptance assay ran and at least one applicable row failed, or could not answer, or no row passed. Only PASS counts toward done; an UNKNOWN row is a row nobody proved (ADR 1115).
gb-048-cause-1 = a FAIL row is the goatnet failing that check; its caught_would_have text says what it means
gb-048-cause-2 = an UNKNOWN row could not be probed from here, most often a goat CLI row run where no goat context with a fabric identity exists (E_PROFILE_MISSING)
gb-048-cause-3 = a SKIPPED negative row whose paired positive did not pass
gb-049-title = the acceptance assay cannot run here
gb-049-what = assay derives the site's values from its group_vars and runs ops/validation/run-acceptance.py with python3; one of those could not start, so nothing was probed.
gb-049-cause-1 = the site name has no ops/ansible/group_vars/<site>_hosts/vars.yml, or --values names no file
gb-049-cause-2 = python3 or PyYAML is not installed
gb-049-cause-3 = the runner stopped with an error rather than a verdict
gb-050-title = nothing is declared on that goatnet
gb-050-what = diff or adopt found no fleet-pins component whose member groups include the goatnet.
gb-050-cause-1 = the goatnet key is right but no component is pinned there yet
gb-050-cause-2 = every component is absent on it
gb-051-title = the plan request is incomplete or not planned yet
gb-051-what = plan writes deploy and rollback plans; a deploy plan needs --env, and a rollback plan reads a host ledger, so it has no fixture form.
gb-051-cause-1 = plan deploy without --env
gb-051-cause-2 = a verb other than deploy or rollback
gb-051-cause-3 = plan rollback with --mock
gb-052-title = that file is not a goatbuilder plan
gb-052-what = apply runs only a file plan wrote, which carries schema goatbuilder.plan/v1.
gb-052-cause-1 = the wrong file was passed to apply
gb-052-cause-2 = the plan was edited by hand and its schema line lost
gb-053-title = the plan no longer matches the tree
gb-053-what = apply recomputed the plan's decisions and at least one moved since it was written; it names each, and runs nothing.
gb-053-cause-1 = main's declaration or the pins file changed since the plan
gb-053-cause-2 = a newer deploy or rollback moved the rollback target
gb-053-cause-3 = the plan is old
gb-054-title = adopt could not create its worktree
gb-054-what = adopt --yes writes its declarations into a fresh worktree of origin/main beside the checkout; git refused to create it.
gb-054-cause-1 = origin/main is not fetched
gb-054-cause-2 = a branch or directory of that name already exists
gb-055-title = a rollback with no --site would flip every goatnet
gb-055-what = the component is deployed on more than one goatnet and no --site was given; with no site the roll narrows to nothing, so it would flip every goatnet at once, prod included. Nothing was touched; the error lists the goatnets to choose from.
gb-055-cause-1 = rollback (or plan rollback) was run on a component that spans several goatnets without --site
gb-055-cause-2 = the goatnet to roll back was left off the command
gb-060-title = a standup input is required and not set
gb-060-what = A role or play the site's standup will reach asserts this variable, or reads it through `| mandatory`, and nothing the site supplies sets it: not group_vars, the inventory, host_vars, the play's vars, a role default, nor an extra var the plan passes. The scan lists every such input at once, before any host is touched.
gb-060-cause-1 = the site's config surface was made from a reference goatnet and this key was not carried over
gb-060-cause-2 = the role gained a required input after the site was configured
gb-061-title = a secret the standup templates is not in this environment
gb-061-what = A value the site supplies is lookup('env', NAME), a play or role the standup reaches uses it, and the declared secrets posture cannot supply NAME (ops/secrets/posture.py, ADR 1204).
gb-061-cause-1 = the run is not under the site's secrets posture
gb-061-cause-2 = the posture's references file has no line for this name
gb-062-title = a standup input may be required
gb-062-what = The input is required under a `when:` condition the scan cannot evaluate from the site's own values (it depends on a registered result, a fact, or a template). This is a warning, never a refusal: the scan does not claim more than it knows.
gb-062-cause-1 = the condition reads a value only known when the play runs
gb-063-title = a standup step's host limit selects no host
gb-063-what = The plan runs this play with --limit <pattern>, and the pattern matches no host of the target group in the inventory, so the play runs on nothing and reports success.
gb-063-cause-1 = the limit assumes the mgmt-<site>-01 host naming and this site names its hosts differently
gb-063-cause-2 = the step installs a service this joining site does not run yet, which its end state names as a gap with the gate row that closes it
gb-064-title = the checkout is behind the trunk in what a deploy reads
gb-064-what = A real run reads ops/ansible (roles, plays, group_vars, inventory, the rendered pins) and ops/pins from the checkout it runs in. The trunk carries commits to those paths that this checkout lacks, so a file rendered from it would remove on the host what those commits added, and the play would report success (contract C3, F-410). Refused before any host is touched, in both avenues (ops/scripts/fresh-source.sh).
gb-064-cause-1 = the checkout was not brought onto the trunk after a merge
gb-064-cause-2 = a feature branch was cut before a change to a role, group_vars or the pins landed
gb-065-title = the trunk could not be fetched, so the checkout cannot be shown current
gb-065-what = A real run proves its checkout is current with the trunk before any host is touched (contract C3). The trunk remote did not answer a fetch, so that proof is missing and the run is refused. An islanded site, cut off by design, runs with --offline: it is judged against the cached trunk and the ledger row it writes says source: offline.
gb-065-cause-1 = no route to the git remote (the laptop is offline, the forge is down)
gb-065-cause-2 = the remote's credentials are not loaded (an ssh key not in the agent)
gb-066-title = the site's declared end state cannot be derived or read
gb-066-what = endstate derives what a site should run from its own declarations (group_vars, the fleet pin table, the inventory) and the service catalog, then reads the site's hosts once over Ansible. One of those could not start, so nothing was graded.
gb-066-cause-1 = the site name has no ops/ansible/group_vars/<site>_hosts/ directory
gb-066-cause-2 = python3 or PyYAML is not installed on this machine
gb-066-cause-3 = the catalog file ops/goatbuilder/endstate/catalog.yaml does not parse
gb-067-title = a site's service presence is undeclared or contradicted
gb-067-what = Every service the catalog lists must be declared present or absent for every governed site, by exactly one answer: a flag in the site's group_vars, the fleet pin table (an absent deviation), or the founder rule. The baseline ops/goatbuilder/endstate/baseline.txt lists the gaps that exist today and may only shrink: a new gap, or a baseline row that is no longer true, fails.
gb-067-cause-1 = a new service in the catalog with no flag declared for a site
gb-067-cause-2 = the fleet pin table renders a component the site's flag says it does not run (or the reverse)
gb-067-cause-3 = a gap was closed and its baseline row was left behind
gb-068-title = the goatnet is not in its declared end state
gb-068-what = endstate graded every service the site declares: present or absent, deployed (its ledger names the declared ref), active (every container running), healthy (its healthcheck or isitup probe), functional (the acceptance rows that exercise it), and its zero-trust edges (its served name resolves and its certificate verifies, its OIDC client is registered in Hydra, its SPIRE entry exists). At least one grade failed or could not be read, or something runs that the end state does not declare (an orphan).
gb-068-cause-1 = a declared service is not running, or its ledger is pending, rolled back or at another ref
gb-068-cause-2 = an edge is broken, such as an OIDC client never registered or a name that does not resolve
gb-068-cause-3 = an orphan, such as a container left behind by a retired service, or a Hydra client or SPIRE entry no service declares
gb-068-cause-4 = a grade could not be read, most often because a host was unreachable or the assay did not run
gb-069-title = the rollout preflight cannot run
gb-069-what = preflight reads the component's declared rollout facts from ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml (owns_state, rollback, restore_from, coupled_with) and the open graduation sets. The component is not in the pins, or the pins could not be read, so there is nothing to grade.
gb-069-cause-1 = the component name is misspelled or not a fleet-pins component key
gb-069-cause-2 = ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml is missing, unreadable, or did not parse
gb-069-cause-3 = on a fixture (--mock), declared/artifacts.yaml did not parse
gb-070-title = survey could read no goatnet at all
gb-070-what = survey reads every goatnet it was asked for in parallel and reports what it could not read as unknown. When not one goatnet answered, there is nothing to compare, and the run stops instead of printing an empty survey that would look consistent.
gb-070-cause-1 = this laptop is on no goatnet's mesh profile and no door opened (no AWS credentials for the SSM door)
gb-070-cause-2 = every goatnet named with --env is misspelled or not in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml
gb-070-cause-3 = ansible is not installed, or ops/scripts/with-aws cannot run
gb-071-title = a site's leaf certificate is not on this controller
gb-071-what = A play that installs a site's leaf (the zenoh router, the LAN mgmt identity, the dashboard pair, the names collector) found the cert or key nowhere it looks: the site manifest's out_dir (<deploy.state_dir>/<site>-leaves), then a copy staged in this checkout or the main checkout. It refuses before touching a host.
gb-071-cause-1 = the leaf was never minted for this site, or was minted on another operator's machine
gb-071-cause-2 = the manifest's out_dir points somewhere this machine does not have
gb-071-cause-3 = GOAT_LEAF_DIR is set to a directory that does not hold it
gb-072-title = goatbuilder cannot name the download surface to update from
gb-072-what = update (and the behind-version notice) reads the release a goatnet serves at get.<site_id>.<site_dns_zone>/goatbuilder/. It looks at GOATBUILDER_UPDATE_URL, then --site (named from the site's group_vars), then the surface the last successful update used. None of them named a surface that serves goatbuilder for this machine.
gb-072-cause-1 = the first update on this machine was run without --site, and GOATBUILDER_UPDATE_URL is not set
gb-072-cause-2 = the site named with --site has no ops/ansible/group_vars/<site>_hosts/ in the checkout goatbuilder was run in
gb-072-cause-3 = the site does not serve goatbuilder (goat_get_goatbuilder_enabled is not true in its group_vars)
gb-072-cause-4 = no goatbuilder release is built for this operating system and architecture
gb-073-title = the goatbuilder download surface could not be read
gb-073-what = update reads version.json, the platform binary and its .sha256 from get.<site>.<zone>/goatbuilder/. One of them did not arrive: the connection failed, the server answered with an error, the answer was not what the surface publishes, or the binary stopped arriving for 30 seconds. Nothing was installed; the binary in place is the one that ran.
gb-073-cause-1 = this machine is not on the goatnet's mesh (get.<site> is mesh-only)
gb-073-cause-2 = the surface is mid-deploy, or does not publish this platform's binary
gb-073-cause-3 = the link stalled; a slow link is fine, a silent one for 30 seconds is not
gb-074-title = the downloaded goatbuilder is not vouched for by its release
gb-074-what = Before touching anything, update checks that the <asset>.sha256 the surface serves agrees with the release's SHA256SUMS, and that the downloaded binary hashes to that digest. One of these failed, so it refused to install; the installed binary is unchanged. The signature over SHA256SUMS is checked in the binary itself; a signature that does not verify is GB-261, and an older goatbuilder reports it as GB-074.
gb-074-cause-1 = the surface is mid-deploy and the binary, its checksum and SHA256SUMS are from different releases
gb-074-cause-2 = the download was altered or truncated on the way
gb-074-cause-3 = the signature over SHA256SUMS is not from the release workflow of the repository goatbuilder.yaml names
gb-074-cause-4 = this goatbuilder was built before the repository move: it expects the signature of the previous release repository, releases are now signed by the current one, and the refusal's expected identity names a different repository from the one it got. Such a binary cannot update itself
gb-075-title = goatbuilder could not replace its own binary
gb-075-what = The download was verified, but writing it next to the running binary or renaming it into place failed. The rename is atomic, so the binary in place is either the old one, whole, or the new one, whole.
gb-075-cause-1 = the install directory is not writable by this user (a binary under /usr/local/bin installed with sudo)
gb-075-cause-2 = the disk is full
gb-076-title = the rollout is not cleanly reversible or not ready
gb-076-what = preflight graded the three rollout facts (State, Rollback, Scope) and the readiness half, and at least one row is not a clean, declared, validated pass. An undeclared fact fails closed (stateful, rollback none) and reads not-clean on purpose, so the gap is visible rather than guessed green; an UNKNOWN is not a pass either. The facts are still printed — a stateful, restore-required rollout is a valid state to deploy knowingly, with --ack.
gb-076-cause-1 = a fact is undeclared, so it failed closed (declare owns_state / rollback / restore_from / coupled_with in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml)
gb-076-cause-2 = a declared rollback contradicts the artifact type (pin-revert over a data/cert/forward-only component, or one flagged reversible false)
gb-076-cause-3 = a readiness check failed — the ref is not pullable, pins --check found drift, the secrets gate is unsatisfied, the checkout is stale, or the end state is not declared
gb-076-cause-4 = a validation could not answer (UNKNOWN) — no registry probe, or no reachable ledger for the rollback --dry-run
gb-100-title = the federation verbs cannot find this party's context directory
gb-100-what = goatbuilder federate init (and verify or apply without --as) reads this party's declaration, federation.toml, and init also reads the mTLS certificate and CA roots beside it. goatbuilder has no profile of its own, so it takes the directory from --context-dir, else $GOAT_CONTEXT_DIR, else the goat profile ($GOAT_PROFILE_DIR, else ~/.goat), and none of those was set.
gb-100-cause-1 = none of --context-dir, GOAT_CONTEXT_DIR, GOAT_PROFILE_DIR or HOME is set, as in a bare container or a service unit
gb-110-title = the inventory does not name a federation host phase 3 can reach
gb-110-what = federate phase 3 measures the edge and the core from this machine, reaching each host the way the peering plays do: the host in the edge group and the host in the core group of inventory/peering-edges.ini, with its address, user, port, key and SSH options. The inventory could not be read, the group has no host or several, or the host uses a connection phase 3 does not use.
gb-110-cause-1 = ansible-inventory is not installed here, or the inventory does not parse
gb-110-cause-2 = the edge group (<partner>_edge) or the core group (<partner>_core) is empty or missing in inventory/peering-edges.ini
gb-110-cause-3 = the group has several hosts and none was named
gb-110-cause-4 = the host uses an ansible_connection other than ssh or local
gb-111-title = a federation host did not answer over SSH
gb-111-what = Before measuring anything, phase 3 runs `true` on the edge and on the core over SSH, with the inventory's connection settings and the plays' privilege escalation. That command failed, so nothing was measured. The message carries the ssh command it ran.
gb-111-cause-1 = this machine is not on a network that reaches the host (mesh profile, door or jump host)
gb-111-cause-2 = the host refused the key (see X-SSH-001)
gb-111-cause-3 = the host key changed (see X-SSH-003)
gb-112-title = sudo on a federation host wants a password
gb-112-what = The host answered over SSH, but `sudo -n true` failed: phase 3 reads the federation interface and the container runtime as root, the way the plays become root, and it never types a password.
gb-112-cause-1 = the host's operator user is not NOPASSWD in sudoers (the baseline play sets it)
gb-112-cause-2 = ansible_become_user names a user this account cannot become without a password
gb-113-title = no core router to measure on the core host
gb-113-what = Phase 3 measures the core router's image and version on the core host. It takes the router the operator names with --router-container, else the name the deployment's variables give (zenoh_stack, or site_id with the goat-zenoh role's default, as <zenoh_stack>-router), else the one running core router discovery finds. That router was not running, could not be read, or several candidates were found.
gb-113-cause-1 = the declared router is not running on the core host, or the variables give a different name than the one deployed
gb-113-cause-2 = nothing declares the router and no running container carries org.goat.component=zenoh-core or a standard name
gb-113-cause-3 = several core routers run on the host
gb-114-title = an edge half of phase 3 cannot be joined
gb-114-what = With two drivers, the edge driver measures phase 3's edge half with `goatbuilder federate init --over-ssh <partner> --side edge --half-out <file>` (or, inside the one command, `goatbuilder federate <partner> --side edge --from 3 --to 3`) and prints its sha256 and a confirm code. The controller joins it with `--side core --edge-half <file> --edge-half-sha256 <code>` (or imports it with `goatbuilder federate import <partner> 3 <file> --sha256 <code>`, which runs the same file checks, and `--from 3` joins it) before it builds and signs the package. The half carries the edge's WireGuard public key, which the package is signed with, so it is joined only when it is the file the edge driver wrote. The file given could not be read; its sha256 is not the one confirmed; it is not an edge half; it was measured for another peering, on another host than this checkout's inventory names as the edge, or for another federation interface than the package asks for; or it was measured more than 24 hours ago, or later than now.
gb-114-cause-1 = the file was changed or not copied whole on its way from the edge driver, or another file was given
gb-114-cause-2 = the confirm code was mistyped, or read from another run of the edge half
gb-114-cause-3 = the edge half was measured for another partner or on another edge host
gb-114-cause-4 = the declaration or the init flags differ between the two machines, so the edge was measured for another interface (or none)
gb-114-cause-5 = the edge half is older than a day, or one of the two machines' clocks is wrong
gb-120-title = a peering names a partner with a reserved word
gb-120-what = A word of `goatbuilder federate` (init lint compose diff verify apply help status plan deploy test allocate backout rotate) can never name a partner: `goatbuilder federate <word>` runs that step, so the peering could never be run by name.
gb-120-cause-1 = a peerings entry's `with:` is one of the reserved words
gb-121-title = two peerings would share a value
gb-121-what = A per-peering value (an interface, a UDP or TCP port, a link subnet, a forwarder port, a certificate directory, an inventory name) is pinned on one peering and already held by another, by a pin or by the allocation record, or a partner is declared twice on one environment. Two peerings on one edge or core host cannot share one.
gb-121-cause-1 = two peerings pin the same value under values:
gb-121-cause-2 = a pin takes a value the allocation record holds for another peering
gb-121-cause-3 = the same partner is declared twice on one environment
gb-122-title = a pinned port is one the edge host already holds
gb-122-what = A pinned WireGuard or carrier listen port is a UDP port the environment's edge hosts already hold (environments[].peering_edge_reserved_udp; absent, NetBird's WireGuard on 51820). A second listener there fails or steals its traffic.
gb-122-cause-1 = values.wg_listen_port or values.carrier_listen_port is pinned to a reserved port
gb-122-cause-2 = a peering running on the role default 51820, on edges with no mesh client, is being adopted
gb-123-title = a peering's pin is not usable
gb-123-what = A pin under a peering's values: is malformed (an interface name over 15 characters, a subnet that is not a /31 or /30 on its boundary, a relative certificate directory, a name with spaces), or pins a forwarder value on a peering no forwarder relays.
gb-123-cause-1 = a typing mistake in a pin
gb-123-cause-2 = public_port or a carrier_* value pinned without forwarder:
gb-124-title = the peering allocation record cannot be used
gb-124-what = ops/goatbuilder/peering-values.yaml, the record of the per-peering values goatbuilder federate allocate chose, is unreadable, has another schema, holds a malformed value, gives two peerings one value, or records a port the edge host already holds. Allocation reads it so a re-run never moves a value; it will not guess around a broken one.
gb-124-cause-1 = a hand edit or a merge conflict in the record
gb-124-cause-2 = a record from a newer goatbuilder
gb-125-title = a peering file exists that goatbuilder did not generate
gb-125-what = goatbuilder federate allocate writes the peering inventory and each peering's peering-values.yml. It never overwrites a file it did not generate (its first line says who did), and never renders a variable a hand-written file already sets for the same hosts (the group, its parent peering group, or the host_vars of its inventory names), because Ansible's file order would then pick the value. Nothing was written.
gb-125-cause-1 = ops/ansible/inventory/peering-edges.ini or a peering-values.yml was written by hand before the values were allocated
gb-125-cause-2 = a hand-written group_vars or host_vars file sets a variable allocate renders (a listen port, a certificate path)
gb-126-title = a peering's inventory group is defined twice
gb-126-what = Not every peering declares hosts:, so ops/ansible/inventory/peering-edges.ini stays hand-written and each peering that declares hosts: gets its own rendered inventory file (inventory/peering-<edge_group>.ini), which the driver reads beside it. That peering's group is also defined in the hand-written inventory, so the plays would see it twice. Nothing was written.
gb-126-cause-1 = hosts: added to a peering whose group is still in the hand-written peering-edges.ini
gb-127-title = no free per-peering value is left
gb-127-what = Every candidate in a per-peering value's sequence is held by another peering of the same environment (128 interfaces, ports or /31 subnets), or by a pin.
gb-127-cause-1 = many peerings on one environment, or pins scattered over the sequence
gb-128-title = a federate run disagrees with the peering's allocated values
gb-128-what = The allocation record holds this peering, and the run's working directory, edge group or core group is not the allocated one. Phase 8 would mint certificates where the edge does not read them, or the plays would run on another peering's hosts. Nothing ran.
gb-128-cause-1 = --work or FED_WORK names a directory whose pki is not the allocated cert_dir
gb-128-cause-2 = --edge-group names another group than the allocated edge_group
gb-128-cause-3 = a partner declared on a second environment, or a core_group pin: the driver runs the republisher on <with>_core and does not read the allocated core group yet
gb-129-title = a peering's offer would widen what federation.toml declares
gb-129-what = federation.toml owns the release sets this party offers ([data] release). A peering's offer: may only narrow that list for one partner. It names a set federation.toml does not offer, is empty or malformed, or federation.toml cannot be read. Or the peering's federation.toml is hand-written (federation_toml: hand), whose [data] release phase 3 offers whole, and offer: is narrower than it; a peering whose federation.toml goatbuilder federate allocate generates gets the narrowed list. Nothing ran.
gb-129-cause-1 = offer: names a set that is not in federation.toml's [data] release
gb-129-cause-2 = offer: is narrower than the [data] release of a hand-written federation.toml (federation_toml: hand), which phase 3 offers whole
gb-129-cause-3 = GOAT_PROFILE_DIR or GOAT_CONTEXT points at another goat context than the one phase 3 runs in
gb-210-title = the host firewall baseline is missing a value it needs
gb-210-what = The host firewall baseline (roles/goat-host-firewall, rendered by its goat-host-firewall-render.py) could not build a ruleset, because a value it derives the rules from is missing or malformed. Nothing was applied.
gb-210-cause-1 = goat_host_firewall_management_sources is empty, so SSH would be admitted from nowhere
gb-210-cause-2 = an edge run reached the role without its peering values (the play was not peering-wg.yml or peering-edge.yml)
gb-210-cause-3 = a port, address or interface name in the input is not one (a partner endpoint given as a name, an interface name over 15 characters)
gb-210-cause-4 = a management source is /0, the mesh interface is the host's uplink, or a br-* bridge that is not docker's (a VXLAN segment bridge) exists while container-bridges is kept
gb-210-cause-5 = twice goat_host_firewall_confirm_timeout plus 15 s is not below goat_host_firewall_rollback_seconds
gb-210-cause-6 = the switch is false but the host still carries the baseline (/etc/goat-host-firewall/profile exists)
gb-211-title = the host firewall override names a rule the baseline does not have, or is malformed
gb-211-what = The deployment's goat_host_firewall_override names a rule that is not in the baseline, names a fixed rule (loopback, established, invalid), uses a key other than drop, sources, ports, interface and reason, asks a fixed-expression rule for more than drop, or gives a reason holding a line break or another control character (the reason is written into the ruleset file). The whole override was refused and nothing was applied.
gb-211-cause-1 = a misspelled rule name, or a peering rule named for an interface this host does not carry
gb-211-cause-2 = an attempt to add a new allow rule; only named baseline rules can be changed, and other-tcp / other-udp are the named rules for a service the host also runs
gb-212-title = the host firewall is loosened without a recorded reason
gb-212-what = An override entry widens a baseline rule (more sources, other ports, another interface), or a role input is wider than the profile (goat_host_firewall_mesh_udp_ports not empty, a mesh DNS port other than 5353, a management source broader than goat_host_firewall_management_prefix_floor, or that floor set broader than /24 or /64), and no reason is recorded for that rule. Tightening is free; loosening a named rule needs a reason recorded in the deployment's configuration. Nothing was applied.
gb-212-cause-1 = the entry has no reason: key, or a blank one
gb-212-cause-2 = an entry meant to narrow widens instead (an empty sources list means any source)
gb-213-title = the host firewall would cut the session managing the host
gb-213-what = The rendered ruleset admits no SSH from the source of the session running the play (read from SSH_CONNECTION on the host), so loading it would lock the deployment out. Nothing was applied.
gb-213-cause-1 = goat_host_firewall_management_sources does not hold the address this play connects from
gb-213-cause-2 = the override drops ssh-management, narrows its sources past this address, or binds it to another interface
gb-213-cause-3 = SSH listens on another port than goat_host_firewall_ssh_port
gb-213-cause-4 = the session's source could not be read, so the check could not run
gb-214-title = the host firewall ruleset was not loaded or not confirmed
gb-214-what = Loading the rendered ruleset failed, or a fresh connection to the host did not succeed after it was loaded. A ruleset that was loaded is undone by the rollback timer the role armed before loading it.
gb-214-cause-1 = the kernel refused the ruleset (nft -c failed), for example an nftables too old for a rule
gb-214-cause-2 = the new ruleset blocks the connection the controller makes, through a path the lockout check could not see (a jump host, a NAT between controller and host)
gb-214-cause-3 = systemd-run is not available on the host, so no rollback could be armed and nothing was loaded
gb-215-title = the host firewall would close a port this host listens on
gb-215-what = Before loading, the host firewall baseline reads the sockets the host listens on (ss) and found one, on a non-loopback address, that no rule admits and that the deployment has not named as closed on purpose. Loading would cut that service without anyone deciding it. Nothing was applied.
gb-215-cause-1 = the host runs another service beside the peering (a management server, a portal, an exporter)
gb-215-cause-2 = a port the deployment means to close is not listed in goat_host_firewall_closed_listeners
gb-180-title = another run of this peering is in progress
gb-180-what = A real run of `goatbuilder federate` holds <work>/.lock from start to end, so two runs of one peering never interleave phases or overwrite each other's run journal. This run found the lock held and changed nothing.
gb-180-cause-1 = another terminal or a scheduled job is running `goatbuilder federate` for the same peering
gb-180-cause-2 = two peerings were given the same working folder (--work or FED_WORK)
gb-181-title = a resource this peering needs is held by another peering on the same host
gb-181-what = Before a peering takes a resource on a shared host (a tunnel interface, a port, a link subnet, a certificate folder), goatbuilder reads every other peering's run journal in the same work root under <root>/.allocation.lock. One of them holds the same resource on the same host, or a journal there could not be read, so nothing was claimed and nothing changed.
gb-181-cause-1 = two peerings were given the same per-peering value (a pinned value in `peerings:` or a hand-written group_vars file)
gb-181-cause-2 = a journal in the work root is damaged, so the check cannot tell what it holds
gb-182-title = the run journal cannot be read
gb-182-what = <work>/state.json is the only account of what ran for this peering, so a journal that does not parse, has a later schema than this goatbuilder reads, or names another peering is refused rather than overwritten. A journal of the same schema that a later goatbuilder wrote is read: a word or field this one does not know is shown as written and kept.
gb-182-cause-1 = the file was edited by hand or cut short
gb-182-cause-2 = a later goatbuilder wrote it with a later schema (the message names the schema and the goatbuilder that wrote it)
gb-182-cause-3 = two peerings share one working folder
gb-183-title = a federate phase failed
gb-183-what = The word the run journal records for a phase that exited non-zero, with its exit code. The run stopped there; no later phase ran.
gb-183-cause-1 = the phase's own error, printed above it (a `goat federate` exit 20 to 24, a play's failed task, a refused gate)
gb-184-title = the working folder or its lock cannot be made, taken or written
gb-184-what = A real run of `goatbuilder federate` keeps its lock and its run journal in the peering's working folder, and the allocation lock in the folder above it; phase 6 keeps there the copy of the package the agreement was composed from, ours.composed.json. One of them could not be created, locked or written, so the run did not start (or, mid-run, its journal is behind, and the run says so in a failed `journal` row; or phase 6 failed, and its row names the copy).
gb-184-cause-1 = the working folder is not writable by this user (the default /var/lib/goat-federation needs root or a folder made for you)
gb-184-cause-2 = the disk is full or the folder is on a read-only mount
gb-130-title = a federation input is missing
gb-130-what = federate phase 1 (preflight) found inputs the later phases need that nothing declared and nothing could derive. Every missing input is listed with where to declare it. Nothing was measured, signed or deployed.
gb-130-cause-1 = the deployment does not declare it (the edge group's group_vars, the peering's entry in goatbuilder.yaml peerings:, the inventory)
gb-130-cause-2 = the script avenue (peering-standup.sh) was run without exporting it; goatbuilder derives what it can, the script does not
gb-131-title = a port this peering needs is already held on the edge
gb-131-what = the tunnel listen port (UDP) or the edge router listen port (TCP) is held on the edge host by something other than this peering's own interface or container. NetBird holds UDP 51820 on a mesh host, which is also the WireGuard default.
gb-131-cause-1 = the tunnel listen port is the WireGuard default 51820 and NetBird's interface holds it
gb-131-cause-2 = another peering's edge router or interface already uses the port on a shared edge host
gb-132-title = the republisher metrics port is already held on the core
gb-132-what = the outbound republisher's loopback metrics port is held on the core host by something other than this peering's republisher. One port per peering on a shared core host.
gb-132-cause-1 = another peering's republisher uses the same metrics port on the core host
gb-132-cause-2 = another service listens on that loopback port
gb-133-title = the edge or core host could not be probed for its ports
gb-133-what = the read-only probe (ops/ansible/peering-preflight.yml) returned no answer for a host, or `ss` could not list its sockets, so the ports were not checked. Not a pass.
gb-133-cause-1 = the host is unreachable with the inventory the peering plays use (mesh down, wrong ansible_host, no SSH key)
gb-133-cause-2 = iproute2 (`ss`) is not installed on the host
gb-134-title = the committed slot list differs from the slot registry
gb-134-what = ops/scripts/sync-slot-anchors.py --check found live slots with no declared store or declared stores for slots that are not live. A core router restarted on this list drops or misses receive stores. Slots minted between the fix and the restart drift it again, so the restart step runs this check once more.
gb-134-cause-1 = slots were minted or revoked since the list was last regenerated and committed
gb-134-cause-2 = the list was regenerated, and a slot was minted before the core router restarted
gb-135-title = the slot registry could not be read
gb-135-what = sync-slot-anchors.py exited 2: the registry was unreadable, or it reported zero live slots. That is not a verdict on drift, and the check is not passed.
gb-135-cause-1 = FED_SLOT_DB names a file that is not the portal registry, or it cannot be opened read-only
gb-135-cause-2 = the management host named by FED_SLOT_PG_SSH is unreachable, or its portal database container differs
gb-136-title = the core has no receive store matching what the edge grants
gb-136-what = the core router's storage_manager has no store whose key expression equals an ingress scope the edge admits (from/<partner>/** by the spec's rule, or the rendered goat_zenoh_edge_ingress_key_exprs). A store wider than the grant, such as from/**, declares a subscriber the edge refuses, so data would cross and not be kept.
gb-136-cause-1 = the core keeps only a historical from/** store
gb-136-cause-2 = the core's per-partner store names a different partner or lane than the edge grants
gb-137-title = the core router's configuration could not be read
gb-137-what = preflight found the core router but could not read its configuration, so the receive stores were not checked. Reported SKIP, never PASS; FED_PREFLIGHT_STRICT=1 refuses on it.
gb-137-cause-1 = the core router reads its configuration from another path than /etc/zenoh/config.json5
gb-137-cause-2 = the account behind FED_ROUTER_DOCKER_HOST may list containers but not exec in them
gb-138-title = no core router container was found
gb-138-what = the selection phase 3 uses (goat federate init) found no running core router on the daemon preflight read: FED_ROUTER_DOCKER_HOST, else the local runtime. Discovery accepts the org.goat.component=zenoh-core label, *-zenoh-router names and legacy goat-zenoh.
gb-138-cause-1 = the core router runs on another host and FED_ROUTER_DOCKER_HOST does not point at it
gb-138-cause-2 = the core router is stopped, or runs under a custom name without the role label
gb-138-cause-3 = the SSH user on the core host cannot use docker there (it is not in the docker group; a goatlite's ssh_user is not): docker over SSH then says 'Cannot connect to the Docker daemon at http://docker.example.com'
gb-139-title = several core router containers were found
gb-139-what = more than one running container qualifies as the core router, and preflight, like phase 3, will not choose among them.
gb-139-cause-1 = an old and a new core router run side by side
gb-139-cause-2 = two stacks on one host both carry a *-zenoh-router name
gb-140-title = our CA certificate is missing or is not a CA
gb-140-what = the certificate phase 8 signs the peering leaves with does not exist, is not a PEM certificate, or is not a CA (basicConstraints CA:TRUE).
gb-140-cause-1 = FED_CA_CERT, goat_federation_ca_cert or the derived ops/ansible/enrollment/ca/<site>/root-cert.pem names a missing file
gb-140-cause-2 = a leaf certificate was named instead of the CA
gb-141-title = our CA signing key cannot be resolved
gb-141-what = the CA private key phase 8 needs is missing, does not decrypt with the age identity, is not a private key, or does not match the CA certificate. The deployment keeps it at <deploy.state_dir>/<site>-ca/ca.key.age; preflight decrypts it in memory to compare and writes no plaintext.
gb-141-cause-1 = the ca.key.age file is not on this machine
gb-141-cause-2 = the age identity (~/.config/age/keys.txt, or FED_AGE_IDENTITY) is not the one the key was encrypted to
gb-141-cause-3 = the key belongs to another CA than the certificate
gb-142-title = our federation signing key cannot be read
gb-142-what = FED_SIGNING_KEY names a file that does not exist, or whose content is neither 64 hex characters (the RFC 8032 seed) nor 44 base64.
gb-142-cause-1 = goat_federation_signing_key or FED_SIGNING_KEY names the wrong path
gb-142-cause-2 = the file holds a PEM or a public key rather than the seed
gb-143-title = the peering inventory does not reach the edge or core
gb-143-what = inventory/peering-edges.ini is missing, has no group for this peering's edge or core, lists no host in it, or does not list the group under [peering_edges:children] / [peering_cores:children]. A play limited to such a group runs against nothing and reports success.
gb-143-cause-1 = the edge or core group was never added to the deployment's inventory
gb-143-cause-2 = the committed inventory ships the host commented out
gb-144-title = the federation declaration or the profile is missing or wrong
gb-144-what = the goat context has no federation.toml, it has no valid sovereign_id or it declares a measured capability, or the profile certificate or CA bundle is missing.
gb-144-cause-1 = `goat profile init` was not run for this context, or ran as another user (sudo -E keeps HOME)
gb-144-cause-2 = the run reads the context `default` (GOAT_CONTEXT unset) while the profile is in another: a goatlite's founder context is named after the goatnet
gb-144-cause-3 = federation.toml was not written, or declares transports, fabric_version, fabric_fips_image or pqc_capable
gb-145-title = the core certificate would not name every address the edge dials
gb-145-what = FED_CORE_SAN does not cover an address the edge's deployment.yml dials. A core certificate without that SAN fails name verification, and nothing is logged at INFO when it does.
gb-145-cause-1 = an endpoint was added to group_vars/<edge group>/deployment.yml after FED_CORE_SAN was set by hand
gb-146-title = docker compose is not available for phase 12
gb-146-what = the edge role deploys with `docker compose`, and this machine has no docker, no compose plugin, or a compose that reports no version.
gb-146-cause-1 = docker-compose-plugin is not installed
gb-147-title = a peering directory value is not usable
gb-147-what = FED_DIRECTORY_URL is not https, or it, FED_PACKAGE_URL, FED_COUNTERPARTY_ID or FED_CA_CERT holds characters outside a plain URL. Those values reach a command line, so the run refuses them.
gb-147-cause-1 = an http:// directory address, or a value with spaces or quotes
gb-148-title = the federation working directory is not writable
gb-148-what = the run's working directory (--work, FED_WORK, else /var/lib/goat-federation/<partner>) cannot be created or written.
gb-148-cause-1 = the default under /var/lib needs root
gb-149-title = the peering is not declared
gb-149-what = the partner is not in goatbuilder.yaml peerings:. A peering nobody declared is one nobody reviewed (contract C1).
gb-149-cause-1 = the peering was never added to the deployment's goatbuilder.yaml
gb-268-title = a peering host's clock is not synchronized, is too far from UTC, or could not be read
gb-268-what = federate phase 1 (preflight) read each edge and core host's clock from its own time service (chronyc -c tracking, else timedatectl). A Zenoh router refuses a timestamp more than 500 ms ahead of its own clock, and the partner's fabric is synchronized separately, so each side must hold its clock within half that, 250 ms by default (FED_CLOCK_BUDGET_MS), of UTC. A host that is not synchronized to an outside reference, or is over the budget, is a FAIL; a host whose clock could not be read is SKIP, never PASS.
gb-268-cause-1 = the host's time service follows no reachable time source, or only the host's own local clock
gb-268-cause-2 = the clock is synchronized but still slewing a large step, or the time source itself is wrong
gb-268-cause-3 = neither chronyd nor systemd answered the probe, so the clock was not read
gb-230-title = allocate would move a peering that already runs
gb-230-what = A peering the allocation record does not hold already runs from hand-written configuration (its edge group has hand group_vars, or the hand inventory names it), and the values allocation chose differ from the ones it runs on (its hand group_vars, else the role defaults). Rendering them would move its tunnel or ports on the next phase 2. Nothing was written.
gb-230-cause-1 = goatbuilder federate allocate run for the first time in a deployment with a peering set up by hand
gb-231-title = the hand-written peering inventory puts one name in two peering groups
gb-231-what = No peering declares hosts:, so ops/ansible/inventory/peering-edges.ini is hand-written, and it puts one inventory name in two peering groups. Ansible merges the group_vars of every group a name is in, so each peering would read the other's rendered values. Nothing was written.
gb-231-cause-1 = one machine listed under the same name for two peerings
gb-234-title = the site inventory does not name one edge and one core for a peering without hosts
gb-234-what = A peering declares no hosts:, so goatbuilder federate allocate takes its machines from the site inventory (the environment's inventory:, else ops/ansible/inventory/*.ini but the peering-*.ini files): the group of the peering's edge_class and of the site's other class, each named as the class, <site>_<class>, <environment>_<class> or <environment>_<site>_<class>. More than one file names them, a class has no machine or several, or the two machines are reached as different users. The message names the candidates and where it looked. Nothing was written.
gb-234-cause-1 = two inventory files define the site's class groups
gb-234-cause-2 = a class group lists several machines, or the edge class group lists none
gb-234-cause-3 = the edge and the core have different ansible_user values
gb-235-title = a named allocate would change another peering's inventory lines
gb-235-what = Every peering declares hosts:, so ops/ansible/inventory/peering-edges.ini is the whole generated peering inventory. `goatbuilder federate allocate <peering>` renders the named peering's groups into it and leaves every other line as it was; this run would change or drop a section of another peering (its hosts were changed in goatbuilder.yaml since the last allocate, or it is declared but not yet recorded). Nothing was written.
gb-235-cause-1 = another peering's hosts: changed in goatbuilder.yaml since the inventory was last rendered
gb-235-cause-2 = a peering in the generated inventory is declared but has no entry in the allocation record
gb-236-title = our core router's goat-zenoh role declarations do not resolve for admission
gb-236-what = Phase 10's admission names no route of its own (FED_CORE_PLAY and FED_CORE_RESTART are unset), and an inventory group of the site inventory (ops/ansible/inventory/*.ini, not peering-*.ini) has group_vars that configure the goat-zenoh role, so admission restarts the core through the role's own play on that group. It could not resolve that play: several groups configure the role and the peering core's address does not pick one, nothing gives the group's site_id, or no single fleet pin whose image variable the group sets names a deploy play that exists. The message names what was looked for and where. Nothing on the core changed.
gb-236-cause-1 = two inventory groups both configure the goat-zenoh role
gb-236-cause-2 = the core router group's group_vars declare no site_id and the group is not named <site>_hosts
gb-236-cause-3 = no component in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml has the group's image variable as its var with a deploy play
gb-237-title = admission cannot write the partner into the role core's zenoh_sovereign_peerings
gb-237-what = On a goat-zenoh role core, phase 10's admission writes the partner's zenoh_sovereign_peerings entry (sovereign, and the CN of the agreement's access-control fragment subject) into ops/ansible/group_vars/<core router group>/peerings.yml, the role's own declaration, beside the fragment. It could not: the fragment names no subject CN or several, another of the group's group_vars files already sets zenoh_sovereign_peerings (two owners), an entry's sovereign is a template (it contains {"{"}{"{"}, {"{"}% or {"{"}#, so no literal id matches it and the partner would be added a second time), peerings.yml cannot be rewritten without changing something else (admission replaces only the zenoh_sovereign_peerings block and keeps every other line of the file byte for byte, so it refuses a comment inside that list, a flow-style top level it cannot split into variables, or an anchor shared between the list and another variable), or peerings.yml is not YAML or not a mapping of variables. Nothing on the core changed.
gb-237-cause-1 = a comment inside the zenoh_sovereign_peerings list of peerings.yml, a flow-style top level, or an anchor in the list that another variable aliases
gb-237-cause-2 = zenoh_sovereign_peerings is set by hand in another file of the core router group's group_vars
gb-237-cause-3 = an entry of zenoh_sovereign_peerings writes its sovereign as a Jinja template, or is not a mapping with a literal sovereign
gb-237-cause-4 = zenoh_sovereign_peerings itself is a template, a mapping or a scalar, not a literal list: read as empty, it would be written back holding this partner alone
gb-237-cause-5 = the fragment phase 9 rendered names no subject, or several
gb-238-title = a federation verb's site flags are given where they do not belong
gb-238-what = goatbuilder federate init|lint|compose|diff|verify|apply read this party's context from --context-dir, --cert, --ca-roots, --context and --profile-dir. Those flags belong to the six verbs and come after the verb: given before it they are read as the name of a partner, and given to a peering run or to `federate plan` they would be ignored.
gb-238-cause-1 = the flags were typed after `federate` and before the verb
gb-238-cause-2 = the flags were given to `goatbuilder federate <partner>` or `federate plan`, which read the deployment's own declarations
gb-239-title = a flag that does not apply to the federation verb it was given to
gb-239-what = The six federation verbs (init, lint, compose, diff, verify, apply) read only the files they are named and the party's context, and run for real. --mock, -C/--repo (except for init --over-ssh) and -o/--output choose things they do not read; --over-ssh and its host flags belong to init alone. A run with one where it does not apply is refused rather than the flag ignored. A flag that belongs to the verb but needs another with it (a host flag on init without --over-ssh) is GB-325.
gb-239-cause-1 = --mock, -C/--repo or -o/--output given to a federation verb
gb-239-cause-2 = --over-ssh or its host flags given to a verb other than init, to a plan or to a peering run
gb-239-cause-3 = --context-dir, --cert, --ca-roots, --context or --profile-dir, which belong to the six verbs, given to a plan or to a peering run
gb-240-title = a federation switch names no choice it has
gb-240-what = Two variables choose how a peering run works: FED_VERBS who runs the federation verbs (goatbuilder, in process, the default; or goat), and FED_MEASURE where phase 3 measures (ssh, the default; or here). Any other value is refused, as peering-standup.sh refuses it, so a misspelling cannot choose a different path on each avenue.
gb-240-cause-1 = FED_VERBS is set to something other than goat or goatbuilder
gb-240-cause-2 = FED_MEASURE is set to something other than ssh or here
gb-241-title = the environment named is not declared
gb-241-what = A verb was given an environment that goatbuilder.yaml (or goatlite.yaml) does not declare in environments:. Nothing runs against an environment nobody declared.
gb-241-cause-1 = a misspelt environment name
gb-241-cause-2 = the environment is declared in another checkout's goatbuilder.yaml
gb-242-title = the command line does not parse
gb-242-what = The arguments do not make a command goatbuilder knows: a required argument is missing, a value is not one the option takes, two options that exclude each other were both given, or the verb is unknown. Nothing ran.
gb-242-cause-1 = a required argument or subcommand was left out
gb-242-cause-2 = an option was given a value it does not take
gb-242-cause-3 = two options that exclude each other were given together
gb-242-cause-4 = a misspelt verb
gb-243-title = a standup names no site
gb-243-what = standup stands up one site and needs --site. It may be left out only on a goatlite run, where goatlite.yaml (preset lite) names the one site the environment has.
gb-243-cause-1 = --site was left out on an environment that is not a goatlite
gb-244-title = the config file cannot be read
gb-244-what = goatbuilder reads the deployment's goatbuilder.yaml (or the file a verb was named) before anything else. It is not there, or it cannot be read.
gb-244-cause-1 = the path is wrong, or -C names another checkout
gb-244-cause-2 = the checkout has no ops/goatbuilder/goatbuilder.yaml
gb-245-title = the config file is not YAML
gb-245-what = The config file could be read, but it does not parse as YAML, so nothing in it can be checked. The message names the line and column.
gb-245-cause-1 = a bracket, quote or indentation error near the line named
gb-247-title = the site or class named is not declared
gb-247-what = standup and join-node act on a site goatbuilder.yaml declares under the environment, and join-node on a class that site declares and that has a type file (ops/goatbuilder/targets/). The one named is not there.
gb-247-cause-1 = a misspelt site or class
gb-247-cause-2 = the site or class is not declared yet
gb-248-title = the observability leg cannot run
gb-248-what = A site that declares has_obs_host: true stands up its observability host in a leg beside the core. --obs-only asked for that leg alone and there is none to run, or the leg's play (ops/ansible/utility-host-bootstrap.yml) is not in this tree. The message says which.
gb-248-cause-1 = has_obs_host is not true, its group_vars or inventory host is missing (--obs-only)
gb-248-cause-2 = the checkout has no ops/ansible/utility-host-bootstrap.yml
gb-249-title = the fixture's standup does not fit what was asked
gb-249-what = standup --mock rehearses the standup a fixture's converger.yaml declares for one environment and site, against that fixture's goatbuilder.yaml. The environment or site asked for is another, or a phase of the rehearsal names a site or class the declaration does not allow (a joiner may touch only the founder it attaches to; a class needs machines).
gb-249-cause-1 = --mock names a fixture for another environment or site
gb-249-cause-2 = the fixture's converger.yaml and goatbuilder.yaml disagree
gb-440-title = a core joined to the founder's own site, which declares no standby pair
gb-440-what = Standup builds a founder site's first core, so a core join-node adds to that site is a second one, and the only second core a site runs is its streaming standby (`intra_site_pair: true`). The site does not declare the pair, so site-bootstrap would skip the standby and the node would come up as a second primary: its own empty NetBird database, and a management service whose OIDC endpoint is a Hydra that runs only on the primary. The run is refused at its first phase (phase 1, or the phase a resume starts at) and phase 13's step is the same refusal, so nothing on the node is changed. A run that stops before phase 13 is not refused.
gb-440-cause-1 = the founder's site is a goatlite, which declares no intra_site_pair
gb-440-cause-2 = the node was meant to be the first core of a new site, and was given the founder's site
gb-441-title = a site's Spock publisher host is also a peer's, so that peer would subscribe to itself
gb-441-what = Each peer subscribes to a site at that site's Spock publisher host, which is spock_mesh_publisher_host or, when it is unset, the host of the site's signal_uri. This site's host is also a peer's, so the peer's subscription would point at the peer itself. spock-mesh-peering stopped before it wired anything. The message names the host and the peer.
gb-441-cause-1 = a joining site's signal_uri names its founder's enrolment name, and the site sets no spock_mesh_publisher_host
gb-441-cause-2 = spock_mesh_publisher_host was copied from another site's group_vars
gb-250-title = a joiner site names no founder
gb-250-what = A site whose vars.yml says goatnet_founder: false joins another site's goatnet, and seeds from it, so it must name that founder in join_founder_site.
gb-250-cause-1 = join_founder_site is missing from the joiner's group_vars/<site>_hosts/vars.yml
gb-251-title = no SSH user is declared for a new machine
gb-251-what = join-node reaches a new machine as the project's declared SSH user before anything is installed on it. The checkout declares none, and no name is right for every project's machines, so a real run refuses (a dry run plans with a placeholder).
gb-251-cause-1 = deploy.ssh_user (or lite.ssh_user) is not set in goatbuilder.yaml
gb-252-title = the partner is declared on several environments
gb-252-what = The same partner is declared as a peering on more than one environment in goatbuilder.yaml peerings:, so the command cannot tell which peering it is about.
gb-252-cause-1 = one partner is peered from two environments, and --env was not given
gb-253-title = a host's back-out survey answered for another peering
gb-253-what = Back-out surveys each host of the peering read-only before it plans what to remove, and every answer names the peering it read. One host's answer names another peering, so nothing is planned from it: removing that peering's resources would be removing someone else's.
gb-253-cause-1 = the inventory groups of two peerings share a host name
gb-253-cause-2 = the survey ran with another peering's working directory
gb-254-title = a file goatbuilder reads or writes cannot be read or written
gb-254-what = A verb reads or writes a file of the checkout or its state (a pin table, a schema, a record, an inventory, a play) and the operating system refused: the file is missing, unreadable, or its directory is not writable. The message names the file and the system's reason.
gb-254-cause-1 = the file is not in this checkout, or -C names another one
gb-254-cause-2 = the file or its directory is not readable or writable by this user
gb-329-title = a file goatbuilder reads from the checkout is not well formed
gb-329-what = A verb read a file of the checkout (a pin table, the ladder, a record, a workflow, the intent schema), and what it holds does not parse as YAML or JSON, or the schema does not compile as JSON Schema draft 7. The file was read: this is not a permission or a missing file (GB-254). The message names the file and the parser's reason, with the line and column where the parser gives them.
gb-329-cause-1 = a hand edit left the file malformed near the line named
gb-329-cause-2 = merge-conflict markers were left in the file
gb-329-cause-3 = the schema file comes from another commit than the rest of the checkout
gb-255-title = nothing is declared for the environment
gb-255-what = The fixture's (or checkout's) declarations name no component for the environment asked for, or none by the name given, so there is nothing to read, plan or roll back.
gb-255-cause-1 = a misspelt environment or component
gb-255-cause-2 = the environment declares no component yet
gb-256-title = a deploy play's hosts cannot be resolved
gb-256-what = To target a deploy, goatbuilder reads the component's play for its hosts: line, following at most three import_playbook hops. The play has neither, or the chain is longer.
gb-256-cause-1 = the play has no hosts: line and no import_playbook
gb-256-cause-2 = more than three import_playbook hops
gb-257-title = fleet-pins.yaml is not in the shape goatbuilder edits
gb-257-what = goatbuilder reads fleet-pins.yaml's components and edits a component's block in place. The file has no components:, or the component's block or its fleet: line is not where the writer looks.
gb-257-cause-1 = fleet-pins.yaml has no components: mapping
gb-257-cause-2 = the component's block was edited by hand into another shape
gb-258-title = a command a verb runs failed
gb-258-what = A verb runs git, gh, ansible or one of the repository's own steps and stops when it fails. The message is that command's own error output; it names what went wrong.
gb-258-cause-1 = the command's own error, quoted in the message (a network failure, a missing credential, a refused push)
gb-259-title = the assembled tree's marker is incomplete
gb-259-what = An assembled checkout (a goatcore commit plus an overlay) carries .goat-assembled, naming the goatcore_ref and overlay_commit it was built from; fresh-source checks against them. A line is missing.
gb-259-cause-1 = the tree was assembled by hand, or the marker was edited
gb-260-title = a rollback has no history to go back to, or its target is refused
gb-260-what = rollback goes back through the host's deploy ledger and the in-repo history (deploy-history.yml). There is no history for the component, not enough of it, or the ref it would roll to is flagged do_not_roll_to, or a host runs a ref neither history knows.
gb-260-cause-1 = the component has not been adopted into deploy-history.yml
gb-260-cause-2 = the history holds fewer entries than the rollback asks for
gb-260-cause-3 = the target ref is flagged do_not_roll_to in the in-repo ledger
gb-260-cause-4 = a host runs a ref no history records (deployed by hand?)
gb-263-title = a site id cannot name the variables its phases read
gb-263-what = Phase 12 reads the NetBird token from <SITE>_NB_API_TOKEN, named after the site that governs the ACL: the site itself, or a joiner's founder (join_founder_site). That site holds a character a variable name cannot, so the standup stops before any phase.
gb-263-cause-1 = join_founder_site in the site's group_vars holds a space, a quote or another character outside letters, digits, - and _
gb-263-cause-2 = the site was named with such a character
gb-264-title = no recorded run matches
gb-264-what = goatbuilder show and runs diff name a run by its id, a prefix of one, or last, from the run records under <state>/runs. None is recorded there, none starts with the prefix given, or several do.
gb-264-cause-1 = no verb that drives hosts has run with this state directory (a --mock run writes no record)
gb-264-cause-2 = a mistyped id, or a prefix too short to name one run
gb-246-title = a rollback depth outside the ledger
gb-246-what = rollback N goes back N deploys in a host's ledger, which keeps the last seven. N outside 1 to 7 names no deploy the ledger holds.
gb-246-cause-1 = N is 0, or more than 7
gb-185-title = the partner named to `federate status` is not a declared peering
gb-185-what = `goatbuilder federate status <partner>` reads a peering declared in goatbuilder.yaml `peerings:` (on `--env`, when given). The name given is not one of them, so nothing was read.
gb-185-cause-1 = the name is misspelled, or the peering is declared under another environment's config
gb-185-cause-2 = the peering has not been declared yet
gb-186-title = the plan was not approved
gb-186-what = `goatbuilder federate deploy` executes a plan only when approval names that plan's hash: `--approve-plan <hash>`, or a person at a terminal typing yes after reading the plan and its hash. `--yes` never approves a plan. No approval was given, or it named another hash, so nothing changed.
gb-186-cause-1 = there is no terminal and --approve-plan was not passed
gb-186-cause-2 = --approve-plan names the hash of an older plan (the plan was made again since)
gb-186-cause-3 = the person answered anything but yes
gb-187-title = the plan is not the one approved
gb-187-what = The plan deploy was given is for another peering, or its hash changed between approval and execution (it is read again under the peering's lock, just before anything changes). Nothing changed. A plan that does not match its own hash is GB-201.
gb-187-cause-1 = no plan has been made yet (<work>/plan.json is missing)
gb-187-cause-2 = the plan was made again, or edited, after it was approved
gb-187-cause-3 = --plan names another peering's plan
gb-188-title = the plan no longer holds
gb-188-what = Immediately before anything changes, deploy runs the planner's live recheck: every live prerequisite is checked again and every input section is compared with a plan built now. Something did not hold (a section changed, a check was not PASS or NOT APPLICABLE or was not answered, or the plan was not READY), so the approved plan no longer describes the change, and a saved plan is not proof the environment stayed the same. Nothing changed.
gb-188-cause-1 = the agreement was composed again (a new package from either side)
gb-188-cause-2 = apply rendered the files again, or one was edited by hand
gb-188-cause-3 = the plan recorded a SKIP of a check deploy does not run again (host-allocation): the inventory names no host for this peering, or a value of another peering on a machine this peering shares does not resolve. The message says which, and what clears it
gb-188-cause-4 = a SKIP the plan accepted (plan --accept) reads differently now: the message names what it reads now and what was accepted
gb-189-title = a stage of `goatbuilder federate` was asked for the wrong way
gb-189-what = The stages `status`, `deploy` and `test` take the partner after them and run their own phases; `--plan` and `--approve-plan` belong to `deploy`, whose start is phase 10 or, once the admission is done by hand, 11. Nothing ran.
gb-189-cause-1 = the partner was left out, or given after a peering name instead of a stage word
gb-189-cause-2 = --to, --resume or a --from other than 10 or 11 was given to a stage
gb-189-cause-3 = --plan or --approve-plan was given outside `deploy`
gb-189-cause-4 = --bilateral was given outside `test`
gb-189-cause-5 = `rotate <partner> <key>` named a key whose rotation is designed and not built in this goatbuilder (`signing`; this goatbuilder rotates the leaves, the carrier keys, the transport key and, on an ipsec-fips peering, the IPsec leaf and CA), or a key it does not know; the message says what is missing
gb-282-title = rotating our CA needs a decision before it can be built
gb-282-what = `goatbuilder federate rotate <partner> ca` would replace the CA that signs our peering leaves. In package v1 every check accepts exactly one CA per party, the one fingerprint our package carries (federation-package-spec.md §5.1), so the partner could not trust the new CA beside the old one while our leaves change over, and data would stop. The overlap of both CAs is decided (ADR 1242: our next CA is announced in package v2) and designed in the key-rotation design §4, but this goatbuilder does not build it yet. Nothing ran.
gb-282-cause-1 = `rotate <partner> ca` was asked for, and this goatbuilder does not yet build the CA overlap ADR 1242 decides
gb-190-title = the admission on our core is not recorded as done for what is rendered now
gb-190-what = `deploy --from 11` skips phase 10, the by-hand admission of the partner's edge on our core, so it runs only when the journal records phase 10 as done for exactly the files rendered now. It does not, so nothing changed: either the admission was never declared, or the rendered files changed after it.
gb-190-cause-1 = deploy has not stopped at phase 10 yet, so the admission was never done for these files
gb-190-cause-2 = the files were rendered again after the admission was declared
gb-191-title = a value for confirmation 1 does not match
gb-191-what = At phase 5 or 6 a value given by flag or typed is not the one the files hold: their CA fingerprint is not their package's, their CA certificate does not carry that fingerprint, their verifying key is not 32 bytes of base64 or does not verify their half, a sovereign id is not a plain id or is not the declared counterparty, the agreement digest differs, or a package offered as the partner's new one is not well formed (its issued date more than 7 days after today's UTC date included), is not signed by the key bound for them, or is not the one --accept-partner-package names (ADR 1229; such a package is set aside as `theirs.incoming.json.refused-<UTC time>`), or, before any phase after 5, the partner's package on disk is not the one confirmed at phase 5, or the keys confirmed there do not hold for it (its CA fingerprint or the keyring's key changed; run phase 5 again), or the package confirmed at phase 5 is no longer at its place (no package held; run phase 5 again with them). The run stopped; at phase 5 no keyring was written and no package was placed, and at phase 6 the composed agreement is left for inspection.
gb-191-cause-1 = a value was misheard or mistyped on the call
gb-191-cause-2 = the partner published a new package or CA after reading theirs aloud
gb-191-cause-3 = the two sides composed different agreements (their halves differ)
gb-191-cause-4 = at phase 6, the counterparty package is not the declared partner's (FED_COUNTERPARTY_ID): compose was given the two parties (--party) and composed nothing
gb-191-cause-5 = a package that is not the partner's current one, signed by another key or replayed, was offered as their new package
gb-192-title = confirmation 1 is waiting
gb-192-what = Phase 5 or 6 was asked to confirm (by --confirm or a value) and something is not there yet: their half or CA certificate has not arrived, a value read aloud was not given and there is no terminal to ask at, the partner has a new package under the key already bound whose digest no one has confirmed (ADR 1229), or the person did not type yes. The run stopped there, as a by-hand stop does (exit 0); a stop at phase 5 writes no keyring.
gb-192-cause-1 = the partner has not published yet
gb-192-cause-2 = one of --partner-key, --partner-ca-fingerprint, --agreement-digest is missing
gb-192-cause-3 = the partner published a new package (after rotating its WireGuard key, for example) and --accept-partner-package is missing
gb-193-title = our verifying key cannot be read, or does not verify our half
gb-193-what = Phase 5 shows our verifying key to read aloud, derived from the Ed25519 seed that signs our package (FED_SIGNING_KEY: 64 hex characters or 32 bytes base64). It is unset, unreadable or not a seed, or the key it gives does not verify the signature on our half, so the partner would bind a key that cannot verify our package.
gb-193-cause-1 = FED_SIGNING_KEY is not set in this environment
gb-193-cause-2 = it names a file that is not the package-signing seed, or a different seed than phase 3 signed with
gb-194-title = the edge cannot be admitted on our core
gb-194-what = Phase 10 by the command (confirmation 2) did not change our core: the agreement digest has not been confirmed with the partner for the agreement in place, --approve-admission names another digest (something changed since it was read), the deployment does not say where our core's access control lives or how it restarts, their CA does not match the agreement, or the fragment could not be merged, assembled or placed (what was placed is put back).
gb-194-cause-1 = phase 6 ran without --agreement-digest (or --confirm), so the digest was never compared
gb-194-cause-2 = the files were rendered again after the summary was read
gb-194-cause-3 = FED_CORE_ACL_ORG, FED_CORE_PLAY or FED_CORE_RESTART, or FED_SLOT_* is not set
gb-194-cause-4 = their CA file holds more than the one certificate the agreement names
gb-195-title = the core's dry run or the slot check refused the admission
gb-195-what = After placing the new access control (nothing loads it before a restart), the core router's dry run failed, or the committed slot list differs from the registry or the registry could not be read. Restarting now could drop or add stores, so the files placed were put back and the core was not restarted.
gb-195-cause-1 = the assembled configuration does not pass the core's play in check mode
gb-195-cause-2 = the slot list in the repository is behind the registry
gb-196-title = the core restart failed after the admission was placed
gb-196-what = The new access control is in place on our core, and the restart through the deployment's play or command failed, so it may not be loaded.
gb-196-cause-1 = the play or restart command failed (its output is above)
gb-197-title = the admission is waiting for approval
gb-197-what = Phase 10 was asked to admit the edge (by --confirm, --approve-admission or --review-in-pr) and stopped without changing our core: the summary was written for review in a pull request, the person did not type yes, or there is no terminal and no --approve-admission. Exit 0, as a by-hand stop.
gb-197-cause-1 = --review-in-pr, or pr at the terminal
gb-197-cause-2 = no approval was given
gb-200-title = the federation plan's inputs changed
gb-200-what = an input the saved plan rests on (versions, the agreement, a rendered file, a certificate, a resource or the acceptance policy) differs from what the plan recorded, so the approval given to that plan does not cover this change.
gb-200-cause-1 = a phase rendered, minted or composed again after the plan was made
gb-200-cause-2 = the checkout, the goat binary or a declared value changed after the plan was made
gb-201-title = the federation plan is missing or altered
gb-201-what = <work>/plan.json is absent, is not a goat-federation-plan/v1 plan, or does not match its own hash, so it cannot stand for what was approved.
gb-201-cause-1 = no plan was made for this working directory
gb-201-cause-2 = the plan file was edited by hand after it was written
gb-202-title = a live prerequisite of the federation plan no longer holds
gb-202-what = rerun immediately before a change, a live phase-1 check (ports, slot list, core router, receive stores) did not pass, so nothing may change. SKIP is not a pass here.
gb-202-cause-1 = another service took a port the plan expected free
gb-202-cause-2 = a slot was minted after the slot list was fixed
gb-202-cause-3 = the core router was replaced or stopped
gb-232-title = a rendered deployment half needs a site value no declaration yields
gb-232-what = The peering declares deployment: rendered, so goatbuilder federate allocate renders the edge's and the core's deployment halves (peering-deployment.yml). Each required site value is taken from the group_vars of the environment's groups (environments[].groups) or all when one sets it, and is otherwise derived from the declaration that owns it: the core's address from peerings[].hosts.core or its line in the peering inventory, the core router's port from goat_zenoh_listen_port (group_vars, else the goat-zenoh role default), the images from the role defaults and ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml, the core's certificate name from its name in the site inventory (ops/ansible/inventory/*.ini). One of them could be found in none of these places; the message names each place it read. Nothing is invented and nothing was written.
gb-232-cause-1 = the peering declares no hosts: and the peering inventory has no single host for its core group
gb-232-cause-2 = no role default and no fleet-pins component pins the image the message names
gb-232-cause-3 = the site values live in a group the environment does not name in environments[].groups
gb-233-title = a peering's federation.toml cannot be generated from the environment's
gb-233-what = The peering declares federation_toml: rendered, so goatbuilder federate allocate generates its federation.toml from the environment's own ops/federation/<environment>/federation.toml, the one owner of sovereign_id, term_days, cut_contact, the release sets, the accept limits and the measured path MTU, and fills [transport] from the allocated values. That file is missing, is not TOML, or lacks a required top-level value. Nothing was written.
gb-233-cause-1 = the environment has no ops/federation/<environment>/federation.toml yet
gb-233-cause-2 = sovereign_id, term_days or cut_contact is missing from it
gb-160-title = two peerings on one forwarder would share a value
gb-160-what = The peerings one forwarder relays must share no carrier interface, public port, carrier port or carrier address on it: the forwarder sends each packet by its port alone. Allocation keeps them apart across every environment the forwarder relays (by its endpoint); this refusal is the backstop for values that collide anyway.
gb-160-cause-1 = a hand edit of peering-values.yaml gives two relayed peerings one forwarder value
gb-160-cause-2 = a pin under values: (public_port, carrier_port, carrier_interface, carrier_subnet) repeats another peering's value
gb-161-title = a peering's partner id cannot name a forwarder entry
gb-161-what = The forwarder names each peering it relays by the partner id, and its role accepts 2-16 characters of a-z 0-9 and hyphen. This partner id is outside that, so the forwarder would refuse the entry.
gb-161-cause-1 = the peering's with: holds an underscore, or is one character or longer than 16
gb-162-title = one forwarder group names two forwarders
gb-162-what = The forwarder's peerings are rendered into its inventory group's group_vars, so every host in the group would relay every peering. Two peerings name the same group with different forwarder endpoints.
gb-162-cause-1 = two forwarders declared without forwarder: group:, so both took the default peering_forwarders
gb-163-title = a forwarded peering has no allocated values yet
gb-163-what = The peering declares forwarder:, so phase 2 runs the forwarder play and copies the carrier keys, and both read the group_vars goatbuilder federate allocate renders from the allocation record. The record does not hold this peering, so those files do not exist. Nothing ran.
gb-163-cause-1 = forwarder: was added to the peering and goatbuilder federate allocate was not run since
gb-339-title = two peerings on one forwarder declare different values for its machine
gb-339-what = A peering's forwarder: may declare the forwarder machine's own values: ansible_user (the user the forwarder play reaches it as), public_interface and ssh_sources. goatbuilder federate allocate renders them into the forwarder group's group_vars, which hold one value of each. Two peerings relayed by the same forwarder group declare different values for one of them, so allocate cannot tell which is the machine's. Nothing was written.
gb-339-cause-1 = the value was changed on one peering's forwarder: and not on the other's
gb-339-cause-2 = two forwarders share one group (forwarder: group:), so their machines' values meet in one file
gb-203-title = two peerings would bind the same value on one machine
gb-203-what = this peering and another declared peering would bind the same WireGuard interface, tunnel UDP port or republisher metrics port, or overlapping link subnets, on the same machine (by inventory address), so one would break the other.
gb-203-cause-1 = two environments, or two hand-named inventory groups, place their edges or cores on one machine
gb-203-cause-2 = a value was pinned or declared by hand without looking at the other peerings on that machine
gb-220-title = federate backout was not given a partner it can act on
gb-220-what = goatbuilder federate backout takes one partner: a sovereign id (2-16 characters of a-z0-9-) that is not a federate subcommand. The working directory and edge group must be plain paths and names, because they go into the play's command line. Nothing ran.
gb-220-cause-1 = the partner was left out, or is not a sovereign id
gb-220-cause-2 = --work or --edge-group holds a quote, a space or a shell character
gb-221-title = the back-out found a resource it cannot show is this peering's alone
gb-221-what = The read-only survey found a resource under this peering's names that another peering holds (a WireGuard interface whose state file or peer names another partner, a carrier another peering's state names), or a core configuration it cannot prove changes by this peering alone (the live file is not seed + org fragment, partner ids overlap, a policy mixes partners). Nothing was changed on any host.
gb-221-cause-1 = two peerings were given the same interface, carrier or stack name in their group values
gb-221-cause-2 = the core's live configuration was edited by hand after the last assemble
gb-221-cause-3 = the core keeps no seed/org split (slot-pool mode), so the admission cannot be located
gb-222-title = the back-out survey did not complete
gb-222-what = ops/ansible/peering-backout.yml failed before every host of the peering answered, or no host answered while the working folder holds the peering's agreement, so there is no plan to show and nothing was changed.
gb-222-cause-1 = a host of <partner>_edge, <partner>_core or peering_forwarders is unreachable
gb-222-cause-2 = neither ops/ansible/inventory/peering-edges.ini nor a per-peering peering-<edge group>.ini names the peering's hosts, so the play matched no host
gb-223-title = the slot recheck did not allow the core router restart
gb-223-what = Immediately before the core router restarts without this peering's admission, the committed slot list is checked against the registry, as the admission path does. It drifted, the registry could not be read, or no registry was named, so the core was not restarted. The edge side of the peering is already removed and the partner has no path to the core.
gb-223-cause-1 = slots were minted or revoked since the list was committed
gb-223-cause-2 = FED_SLOT_DB (or FED_SLOT_REGISTRY_STORE=postgres with FED_SLOT_PG_SSH) is not set for a deployment with lazy slot storage
gb-224-title = the core router was not restarted on the reviewed candidate
gb-224-what = The core step installs only the candidate configuration whose digest the survey printed. The core's files changed since the survey (so the candidate's digest differs), more than one core router was found, or the restart failed. The org fragment kept beside it as <fragment>.before-backout-<partner> is the state before the step.
gb-224-cause-1 = another change reached the core between the survey and the restart
gb-224-cause-2 = several containers qualify as the core router
gb-225-title = resources of the peering are still present after the back-out
gb-225-what = The survey run after the removal still finds resources of this peering, or the removal play stopped partway. Every step is idempotent: running the back-out again removes what is left and reports the rest as absent.
gb-225-cause-1 = a host became unreachable during the removal
gb-225-cause-2 = a unit or container would not stop
gb-226-title = our package for the counterparty could not be withdrawn from the directory
gb-226-what = The back-out asks the peering directory to delete /v1/parties/<us>/packages/<partner>, signed by our bound key. The directory refused or could not be reached. This is a warning: a listing grants nothing, and the peering's hosts are already clean. Our single entry and every other party's entry are never touched.
gb-226-cause-1 = FED_SIGNING_KEY is not set, or is not the key the directory binds to us
gb-226-cause-2 = the directory is unreachable
gb-227-title = the core still admits the partner through its own configuration
gb-227-what = The core router was configured by roles/goat-zenoh from zenoh_sovereign_peerings, which declares the partner's subject and grants as sovereign-<partner> ids. The back-out takes the partner's entry out of group_vars/<core router group>/peerings.yml, the file admission writes it into, and runs the role's play, which restarts the router without the partner's store and then deletes that store whole (ADR 1234). After the back-out the core still declares the partner: the entry is in a file admission does not write, the back-out could not rewrite peerings.yml (GB-237), the core was admitted through the deployment's own route (FED_CORE_*), or the role's play failed. The partner has no edge left to reach the core through.
gb-227-cause-1 = the core admits the peering from a zenoh_sovereign_peerings entry the back-out did not write, or could not take out
gb-227-cause-2 = the role's play failed after the entry was taken out, and the entry was put back
gb-270-title = a backed-out partner's store was kept because the router does not yet run without it
gb-270-what = On a goat-zenoh role core, the back-out took the partner out of zenoh_sovereign_peerings, ran the role's play to restart the router without the partner's store, then ran ops/ansible/zenoh-drop-partner-store.yml, which deletes <storage path>/sovereign_from_<partner> only after the router runs without it (ADR 1234). One of the two plays did not get there: the partner is still declared, the live configuration still names the store, the router is not running, or it started before that configuration was written. The store was kept, and the back-out put the partner's entry back in peerings.yml, so running it again repeats the step.
gb-270-cause-1 = the role's play failed before or during the router's restart
gb-270-cause-2 = the router did not restart after the configuration without the store was written
gb-270-cause-3 = the partner is declared in another of the core router group's files
gb-276-title = --mock was given to a verb that reads the checkout
gb-276-what = --mock runs a verb against a fixture directory instead of hosts. Only the verbs that drive or read hosts take one (the message lists them). This verb reads the checkout itself, so a fixture it was named would have been ignored, and the run would have reported on files the person never pointed it at. Nothing ran.
gb-276-cause-1 = --mock given to a verb that reads the checkout, such as pins, validate or doctor
gb-277-title = an edge result cannot be imported
gb-277-what = On a two-driver peering (drivers: two), the controller imports the file the edge driver wrote for a phase's edge half with `goatbuilder federate import <peering> <phase> <file> --sha256 <code>`, the confirm code the edge driver printed. The import refused it: the file cannot be read; the code is not hex, gives fewer than the 32 hex digits the edge driver prints, or is not the start of the file's sha256; the file is not that phase's edge result for this peering (another schema, another peering, another side); or this peering's journal here is not waiting on the edge driver at that phase. Nothing was placed or recorded.
gb-277-cause-1 = the file was changed, or not copied whole, on its way from the edge driver
gb-277-cause-2 = the confirm code was mistyped, or read from another run of the edge driver
gb-277-cause-3 = the file is another peering's, another phase's, or the core side's result
gb-277-cause-4 = the controller's run has not reached the phase's hand-off in this working directory
gb-286-title = a joining site has no leaf-cert manifest join-node can read
gb-286-what = join-node mints a joining site's router and service leaves from the site's manifest (ops/enrollment/manifests/<site>-certs.yaml, with mint-site-certs.sh) and stages them onto the node before the data router. The manifest is missing, or is not readable YAML, so the plan was refused before any phase ran.
gb-286-cause-1 = the site's manifest was never written
gb-286-cause-2 = the manifest has a YAML error
gb-287-title = a joining site's leaf-cert manifest has no router leaf
gb-287-what = join-node deploys a joining site's data router (zenoh-deploy.yml), whose play reads the router leaf that mint-site-certs.sh mints from the site's manifest. The manifest names no leaf called router, so the plan was refused before any phase ran.
gb-287-cause-1 = the manifest was written without the router entry ops/enrollment/manifests/TEMPLATE-certs.yaml carries
gb-288-title = a health publisher a joining site declares has no play in the checkout
gb-288-what = join-node deploys each health publisher the end-state model marks present at a joining site (endstate.py model <site>, from the pin table) after the site's data router, by running the publisher's play limited to the node. The model marks a publisher present whose play (ops/ansible/<publisher>-deploy.yml) is not in this checkout, so the plan was refused before any phase ran.
gb-288-cause-1 = the checkout is an older or partial tree that does not carry the publisher's play
gb-288-cause-2 = the pin table declares a publisher at the site that the site does not mean to run
gb-296-title = a site turned on the audit spool, which is not built
gb-296-what = A site set audit_subscriber_spool_enabled: true. The spooling audit subscriber a phone site needs (docs/design/island-mode-audit-spool.md) is designed but not built, so there is nothing to deploy for it. The audit-subscriber role stopped before it changed anything.
gb-296-cause-1 = a phone site's group_vars set audit_subscriber_spool_enabled: true before the spool exists
gb-293-title = the back-out cannot tell what our core router's leaf held before this peering
gb-293-what = Our core router's leaf (its certificate and key) holds the leaf this peering's admission placed, and no admission run in the peering's working folder recorded what was there before (both peerings admitted by hand, say), so the back-out can neither put it back nor remove it. No other peering is set up from this machine (no working folder beside this one holds an agreement); when one is, the leaf is kept for it and the back-out goes on. Nothing was changed on any host.
gb-293-cause-1 = the leaf was placed by hand, not by goatbuilder federate admission
gb-293-cause-2 = the admission folder of the peering's working folder was removed or moved
gb-280-title = a joining site has no mesh DNS zone to serve
gb-280-what = join-node deploys a mesh DNS replica on a joining site's core node, so the site still resolves the goatnet's names when it is cut off from its founder (ADR 1235). The replica serves the goatnet's generated zone, an ops/dns/<dir>/mesh-hosts whose records carry the site's site_dns_zone (the founder's when the site declares none). No such file was found, or neither site declares site_dns_zone, so the plan was refused before any phase ran.
gb-280-cause-1 = the goatnet's zone was never generated or committed (ops/dns/generate-mesh-hosts.py)
gb-280-cause-2 = neither the joining site's nor the founder's group_vars declare site_dns_zone
gb-281-title = the founder's management API token for mesh DNS is unset or unresolved
gb-281-what = join-node's mesh DNS step registers the joining site's nameserver group through the founder's management API, with the token in <FOUNDER>_NB_API_TOKEN. After the secrets injector ran, the variable was empty or still held a secret store's reference, so the step was refused before it registered anything. The message names the variable, never its value.
gb-281-cause-1 = the variable is not stored under the declared secrets posture
gb-281-cause-2 = the store's tool is not signed in, so with-secrets could not resolve the reference
gb-299-title = no phone, or no Linux VM on it, answers over the Android debug bridge
gb-299-what = join-node --via usb reaches a phone node through the Android debug bridge (adb) on a USB cable: SSH is carried by `adb shell nc <the VM's address> 22`, and the address is looked up on every connection (ops/scripts/phone-node-ssh.sh). adb is not installed, lists no phone as `device`, the phone is locked, or the phone's Linux VM has no address. Nothing reached the node.
gb-299-cause-1 = the phone is not attached, USB debugging is not allowed on it, or this machine's key was not accepted on its screen
gb-299-cause-2 = the phone is locked
gb-299-cause-3 = the Terminal app is closed, so the VM is not running
gb-299-cause-4 = more than one device is attached and ANDROID_SERIAL does not name the phone
gb-299-cause-5 = adb (Android platform tools) is not installed on this machine
gb-300-title = the phone's Linux VM is not a host a node can be built on
gb-300-what = For a phone node, phase 2 of join-node prepares the phone's Linux VM (Debian's kernel with its boot settings, Docker Engine, the deploy user) and then runs the integrity checks in the VM (ops/scripts/phone-node-provision.sh, ops/scripts/phone-node-integrity.sh). The VM could not be prepared, did not come back on Debian's kernel, or failed a check; the line before this one names which. No later phase ran, so nothing is built on the VM.
gb-300-cause-1 = the VM started again on the kernel Android supplies, or without the boot settings
gb-300-cause-2 = file reads differ between cache drops, a write does not read back the same, or an installed file fails dpkg --verify: the VM returns wrong file data
gb-300-cause-3 = the kernel log holds a filesystem or I/O error
gb-300-cause-4 = the WireGuard module does not load, or a container does not run
gb-300-cause-5 = the phone does not support a VM, or has no Linux Terminal app
gb-301-title = a site does not hold what the machine class phone declares
gb-301-what = The machine class phone declares once, in ops/ansible/group_vars/class_phone.yml, what a phone node differs in: no LAN address, no public ingress, no disk encryption inside the VM, memory limits for 4 GB, no observability host. The config preflight (standup-config-preflight.py <site>_hosts --class phone, phases 3, 7 and 12 of join-node) found the site at odds with it: its group_vars set one of the class's keys to another value or set a key the class rules out, its node is not under [class_phone] in inventory/mgmt-hosts.ini or has a LAN address there, or the class file is not in the checkout. The same code refuses --class phone at a site that does not declare goatnet_founder: false, before any phase.
gb-301-cause-1 = the site's group_vars carry a value copied from a site of another class
gb-301-cause-2 = the node's line was not added under [class_phone] in ops/ansible/inventory/mgmt-hosts.ini
gb-301-cause-3 = the node's line in ops/ansible/inventory/mgmt-hosts.ini carries an ansible_host
gb-301-cause-4 = the site is a founder: a phone node joins a goatnet
gb-297-title = a joining node's clock is not synchronized or is too far from UTC
gb-297-what = join-node phase 3 read the joining node's clock from its own time service (chronyc -c tracking, else timedatectl), before anything on the node changed. A Zenoh router refuses a timestamp more than 500 ms ahead of its own clock, and nothing tells the machine's owner, so the node must hold its clock within half that, 250 ms by default (JOIN_NODE_CLOCK_BUDGET_MS), of UTC. A node that is not synchronized to an outside reference, or is over the budget, is refused; a node whose clock could not be read is reported as not verified, never as passed. The rule is the federation preflight's check clock (GB-268).
gb-297-cause-1 = the node's time service follows no reachable time source, or only the node's own local clock
gb-297-cause-2 = the clock is synchronized but still slewing a large step, or the time source itself is wrong
gb-304-title = grow was given a file that is not a goatlite
gb-304-what = goatbuilder grow hands a goatlite over to the full preset, so it reads goatlite.yaml at the checkout root and needs it to declare preset: lite with a lite: block (ADR 1213). This file declares another preset, or has no lite: block, so there is nothing to grow. A checkout already on the full preset needs no grow. grow changes files in the checkout only, never a host: a finished grow is undone with git before it is committed (restore goatlite.yaml and the files whose generated header it took off, and delete ops/goatbuilder/goatbuilder.yaml), and goatbuilder grow --dry-run shows every write first.
gb-304-cause-1 = goatlite.yaml declares preset: full, or no preset
gb-304-cause-2 = the lite: block is missing or is not a mapping
gb-305-title = grow would replace a configuration that already exists
gb-305-what = goatbuilder grow writes the full configuration to ops/goatbuilder/goatbuilder.yaml. That file already exists and holds another configuration (not the one this goatlite grows into, which a run that stopped part way would have left), so grow refused before changing anything: it never replaces a configuration someone wrote. When the file carries grow's own header, the message says it is grow's file from an earlier run, since edited (or goatlite.yaml changed). grow changes files in the checkout only, never a host: a finished grow is undone with git before it is committed (restore goatlite.yaml and the files whose generated header it took off, and delete ops/goatbuilder/goatbuilder.yaml), and goatbuilder grow --dry-run shows every write first.
gb-305-cause-1 = the checkout already declares a full configuration of its own
gb-305-cause-2 = the file was edited after an earlier grow wrote it, and goatlite.yaml is still there
gb-306-title = the full configuration grown from a goatlite is incomplete
gb-306-what = goatbuilder grow rewrote goatlite.yaml as a full configuration, and the result fails the schema or a cross-field rule, so it was not written. Most often the goatlite is the four-fact file of the tutorial: the lite preset took repo and components from the checkout, and a full configuration declares its own. The message lists each problem and names the fields to add. grow changes files in the checkout only, never a host: a finished grow is undone with git before it is committed (restore goatlite.yaml and the files whose generated header it took off, and delete ops/goatbuilder/goatbuilder.yaml), and goatbuilder grow --dry-run shows every write first.
gb-306-cause-1 = goatlite.yaml declares no repo or components, and the checkout has no configuration to take them from
gb-306-cause-2 = a value in goatlite.yaml is valid for the lite preset but not in the full shape (the message names it)
gb-307-title = an environment declares converger pull-agent, and the pull converger is not built
gb-307-what = goatbuilder.yaml declares converger: pull-agent for this environment, one of its sites or one of their classes. No pull converger is built: no agent on a machine fetches and applies its class's release. The only converger on real hosts is push, and pushing to machines declared to pull would change what the declaration means once pull is built. So doctor refuses the environment, and deploy (its dry run too), release's deploy rungs, fastlane's deploy and rollback refuse it before any step. deploy --verify-only reads and is not refused; validate accepts the value, and --mock converges it on a fixture.
gb-307-cause-1 = the environment, a site or a class was declared pull-agent ahead of the pull converger
gb-320-title = a program the run starts could not be started
gb-320-what = A goatbuilder verb runs its phases and helpers as programs: bash for a phase's command, python3 for a step written in Python (the preflight, the slot recheck), the tools a verb drives (ansible, ssh, git, go) and the helpers a step names (the core's candidate planner, a script of the checkout such as ops/scripts/fresh-source.sh or an overlay's assemble.sh). The operating system refused to start one: it is not installed, not on PATH, or not executable. Nothing of that step ran.
gb-320-cause-1 = bash, python3, ansible, ssh, git or go is not installed on the machine goatbuilder runs on, or not on PATH
gb-320-cause-2 = a helper the step names is missing from the checkout, or has lost its executable bit
gb-321-title = a file a step of this run wrote is missing or is not what it wrote
gb-321-what = A step of a federate run leaves its result in the working folder for the next one to read: phase 1's preflight result and the slot recheck's, the agreement, a back-out host record, the rotation id, an edge result on a two-driver peering. The file the next step read was not there, was not JSON, or did not hold what that step writes (another peering's name, a malformed id). Nothing after it ran.
gb-321-cause-1 = the step that writes it stopped before writing it (its own failure is above, or in its log)
gb-321-cause-2 = the file was edited, replaced or removed by hand between the steps
gb-321-cause-3 = a different peering's working folder was named (--work, FED_WORK)
gb-322-title = goatbuilder reached a state its own code rules out
gb-322-what = goatbuilder checked one of its own assumptions and found it broken: a plan whose shape the code that builds it no longer matches, a verb dispatched to a place that never takes it, a rotation marked built with no run. No input of yours can cause this; it is a defect in this build. Nothing after the check ran.
gb-322-cause-1 = a defect in this goatbuilder build
gb-323-title = a file the run writes outside the working folder cannot be written
gb-323-what = Some steps write outside the working folder: the back-out deletes the partner record it generated from the checkout, and init --side edge writes the edge half to the file --half-out names. That file could not be written or removed.
gb-323-cause-1 = the checkout or the folder --half-out names is not writable by this user
gb-323-cause-2 = the disk is full, or the folder is on a read-only mount
gb-324-title = a setting in the environment has a value goatbuilder does not take
gb-324-what = A goatbuilder setting read from the environment (a FED_* variable) has a value outside the ones it takes; the message names the variable and the values it takes. Nothing ran.
gb-324-cause-1 = the variable was set by hand, or by an older script, to a value this build does not know
gb-325-title = flags given to a federate stage or verb do not go together, or one they need is missing
gb-325-what = A federate stage was given flags it cannot take together (--accept with --recheck, --recheck with --mock or --no-live, --side without the files that side reads or writes), a flag without the one it needs (--work for status without the partner, init --over-ssh without -C, a host flag such as --edge-host or --side on init without --over-ssh), or a flag naming something the saved plan does not hold (--accept <check>), or a value the saved plan was made with differently (--edge-group or --as on a recheck). Nothing ran.
gb-325-cause-1 = a command line put together from two different runs' flags
gb-315-title = a program a step ran gave output or an exit goatbuilder cannot use
gb-315-what = A step of a federate run started a program and reads its answer: the core's candidate planner prints a plan on stdout, and phase 1's preflight exits and writes a result whose ready verdict must agree with its exit. The program ran, but what it gave cannot be used: no plan on stdout, or an exit and a result that disagree or cannot be read. Its own words are in the message. Nothing after it ran.
gb-315-cause-1 = the program failed before it gave its answer (its stderr is in the message)
gb-315-cause-2 = the program is a different version from the one this goatbuilder expects
gb-316-title = a confirmation could not be read from the terminal
gb-316-what = A federate stage asked a person to type a confirmation (the back-out asks for the partner id) and reading the answer from the terminal failed. Nothing was changed.
gb-316-cause-1 = the terminal closed, or stdin is not readable
gb-326-title = a file of the checkout goatbuilder reads is not the YAML it should be
gb-326-what = goatbuilder read a file of the checkout (fleet-pins.yaml, the inventory's doors.yaml, a role's deploy-history.yml, ops/goatbuilder/teardown.yaml) and it does not parse as YAML, or not in the shape that file has. The message names the file and the parser's line and column. Nothing that reads it ran.
gb-326-cause-1 = a bracket, quote or indentation error near the line named
gb-326-cause-2 = the file was edited by hand into another shape
gb-331-title = a standup phase failed
gb-331-what = A phase of goatbuilder standup exited non-zero. The row prints the phase, its label and its exit code; the run stopped there, and no later phase ran. The site is left partway through that phase.
gb-331-cause-1 = the phase's own error, printed above the row (a play's failed task, a refused gate, a host that did not answer); the interpreted row under it names a catalog code when the output carries one
gb-332-title = a join-node phase failed
gb-332-what = A phase of goatbuilder join-node exited non-zero. The row prints the phase, its label and its exit code; the run stopped there, and no later phase ran. The node is left partway through that phase.
gb-332-cause-1 = the phase's own error, printed above the row (a play's failed task, a node that refused our key, a refused gate); the interpreted row under it names a catalog code when the output carries one
gb-333-title = an image pull failed
gb-333-what = goatbuilder standup pulls the pinned images beside phases 3 and 4, and the pull exited non-zero. The row prints its exit code and its log, which names each image that could not be pulled. The phase that first runs those images, and every phase after it, did not run.
gb-333-cause-1 = a private package and no GHCR_READ_TOKEN on the laptop (phase 2 logs the box in with it)
gb-333-cause-2 = a pin that names an image the registry no longer has
gb-334-title = an observability leg failed
gb-334-what = A site that declares has_obs_host: true stands up its observability host in a leg beside the core phases, and that leg exited non-zero. The row prints the leg, its exit code and its log. The core phases before the one that needs the leg have run; that phase and the later ones did not.
gb-334-cause-1 = the leg's own error, in its log (obs_scrape_targets unset is the usual one for the stack leg)
gb-335-title = a teardown failed
gb-335-what = goatbuilder teardown stops a site's services in reverse order, and one stop exited non-zero. The row names the component and the exit code. The services before it are down; nothing after it ran.
gb-335-cause-1 = the stop's own error, printed above the row (a compose project or unit that did not stop, a host that did not answer)
gb-278-title = the edge driver's checkout or carried files are not the controller's
gb-278-what = On a two-driver peering (drivers: two), the edge driver runs a phase's edge half with the command the controller's hand-off names: `goatbuilder federate <peering> --side edge --from <N> --to <N> --expect-commit <commit>`, with one `--expect <file>=<sha256>` per file the controller carried. The edge driver refused to run: --expect-commit was not given; its checkout is not a git checkout, has no commit, has changes, or is at another commit than the one named; or a carried file is not under its <work> or in its checkout, or has another sha256. Nothing ran on the edge.
gb-278-cause-1 = the edge driver's checkout was not updated to the controller's commit, or has local changes
gb-278-cause-2 = the command was typed without the hand-off's --expect-commit
gb-278-cause-3 = a carried file was not copied from the controller's <work>, or was changed on the way
gb-314-title = the node does not answer at join-node's phase 1
gb-314-what = join-node phase 1 checks the node answers before anything is done on it: over SSH at --address (one batch-mode connection with this machine's ssh, a 10 s connect timeout and no password prompt), or for a phone node over the Android debug bridge (adb get-state). A node whose SSH server refuses the login has answered: before phase 2 seeds this machine's key, a password-only node refuses every batch login. This node did not answer: the connection never reached its SSH server (no route, refused, timed out, a name that does not resolve, a changed host key), or no single phone answers over USB. Nothing was done on the node and no later phase ran.
gb-314-cause-1 = --address is not the box's address on the site LAN, or the box is off or not yet on the network (on a Jetson, 192.168.55.1 is the USB device-mode bridge, reachable only over a direct USB-C cable)
gb-314-cause-2 = the box's host key changed since this machine last connected (ssh names it), or the address is a name this machine cannot resolve
gb-314-cause-3 = for a phone: it is not attached, USB debugging is not allowed for this machine, its screen is locked, or more than one device is attached without ANDROID_SERIAL
gb-310-title = the package held for a partner cannot be read, so it cannot be recorded as confirmed
gb-310-what = A peering whose phase 5 was confirmed by a goatbuilder that did not record the partner's package (before ADR 1229) has its held package recorded as the confirmed one on its first run under this build, before any phase: journaled as a migration, not as a person's confirmation, so a package older than it is refused and compose checks it. The package held for <partner> at <work>/theirs.canonical.json could not be read, so nothing could be recorded, and the run stopped rather than going on unprotected. Nothing ran.
gb-310-cause-1 = <work>/theirs.canonical.json was removed or moved, or is not readable by this user
gb-308-title = the partner's package offered is older than the one we hold
gb-308-what = At phase 5, a package from the partner signed by the key already bound was offered as their new one (by the directory step at <work>/theirs.incoming.json, or copied over <work>/theirs.canonical.json by hand), and its `issued` date is before the one of the package we hold, or it has no readable `issued` date while the one we hold has (ADR 1229). `issued` is the only member that orders a party's packages, so an older one is a replay or a rollback. It is refused, even with --accept-partner-package and its own digest, so a replay is never asked about and approved by habit. Nothing was placed; a file from the directory step was set aside as theirs.incoming.json.refused-<UTC time>.
gb-308-cause-1 = a directory, or a copy on the hand-exchange path, served an older package the partner once signed
gb-308-cause-2 = the partner rolled back to an earlier package without publishing it again
gb-289-title = the IPsec rotation's staged material is missing or unreadable
gb-289-what = A credential rotation of an ipsec-fips peering (goat-peering-edge with peering_edge_rotation set) switches the edge to a new leaf that the offline ceremony issued and an operator staged beside the live files: <name>-cert.next.pem in the swanctl x509 directory, <name>-key.next.pem in its private directory at mode 0600, and for an ipsec-ca rotation our new CA as <name>-local-ca.next.pem in x509ca. The rotation also needs our current IPsec CA certificate at peering_edge_local_ca_path to check the new leaf's chain. One of them is missing, unreadable, or the key is not mode 0600. The role refused before anything moved.
gb-289-cause-1 = the new leaf, its key, or (for ipsec-ca) our new CA was not staged on this edge
gb-289-cause-2 = peering_edge_local_ca_path is not set, or names no file on the edge
gb-289-cause-3 = the staged key was copied without mode 0600
gb-290-title = the staged IPsec certificate is not right for this link
gb-290-what = Before an IPsec credential rotation moves anything, goat-peering-edge checks the staged certificate: its key is the staged key, it chains to the CA it must chain to (our current CA for ipsec-leaf, the staged new CA for ipsec-ca), its subject CN is peering_edge_local_id, its key is ECDSA P-384 (secp384r1) as the proposals require, it is valid now, it stays valid for peering_edge_min_valid_days more days (default 30), and its key is not the live leaf's (the confirm tells the two leaves apart only by their keys, so a leaf re-certified on the old key is refused). The message names each check that failed. Nothing was moved.
gb-290-cause-1 = the leaf was issued for another peering or another identity
gb-290-cause-2 = the leaf was issued by another CA, or the staged key belongs to another certificate
gb-290-cause-3 = the leaf uses another key type, or expires too soon
gb-290-cause-4 = the leaf was re-certified on the live leaf's key instead of a new one
gb-295-title = a cut-off site's access-rule run would have created a group, or created or deleted a policy
gb-295-what = ops/ansible/netbird-acl-cutover.yml or ops/ansible/netbird-acl-bootstrap.yml ran with cut_off=true, for a site cut off from the others. The flag is the operator's statement; nothing detects a cut. While cut off, a site may only update the access-rule policies it already has, by their ids, and may create no group: NetBird assigns a created policy's or group's id, so two cut-off sites that each create the same rule or group leave two of them after the heal, and a delete at one site is undone or doubled by the other. This run would have created a group, or created or deleted a policy (the message names each group and policy it would have created and each id it would have deleted), so it stopped before it wrote anything, updates included.
gb-295-cause-1 = the access-rule matrix names a rule the site has no policy for yet
gb-295-cause-2 = the group taxonomy names a group the site does not have yet (netbird-acl-bootstrap.yml, which standup runs before the cutover)
gb-295-cause-3 = reap_orphans=true, and the site holds a generated policy the matrix no longer names
gb-295-cause-4 = enforce with the Default policy action delete
gb-295-cause-5 = the teardown play, which only deletes
gb-302-title = the IPsec link did not come up on the new leaf; the old pair was put back
gb-302-what = After switching to the staged leaf, goat-peering-edge loaded the credentials, re-authenticated the IKE SA and waited up to peering_edge_rotation_confirm_seconds (the DPD timeout by default) for an established IKE SA with an installed CHILD SA. None came up, so it put the set-aside pair back from .previous-<id>/, loaded it and re-authenticated: the old leaf is live. It marks the rotation rolled back (.previous-<id>/rolled-back), so every later run of the same id, a resume or a retire, reports GB-302 again, moves nothing and never replaces our CA. For ipsec-ca with --no-overlap, a partner that no longer trusts our old CA cannot carry the link on the old pair either.
gb-302-cause-1 = the partner does not trust the CA that issued the new leaf
gb-302-cause-2 = the partner's edge expects another identity, or is not reachable
gb-302-cause-3 = this rotation id was rolled back by an earlier run
gb-303-title = the partner has not read back our new IPsec CA
gb-303-what = An ipsec-ca rotation switches the edge to a leaf issued by our new per-peering CA only once the partner trusts that CA. Nothing on our side can read the partner's trust store, so the switch needs the sha256 fingerprint the partner read back after placing our new CA (peering_edge_rotation_ca_readback, or goatbuilder federate rotate <partner> ipsec-ca --partner-holds-ipsec-ca <sha256>). It was not given, or it is not the staged CA's fingerprint. Nothing was moved.
gb-303-cause-1 = the partner has not placed our new CA yet, or has not read its fingerprint back on the call
gb-303-cause-2 = the fingerprint read back belongs to another file
gb-298-title = an IPsec rotation under way, or not yet retired, is not the one asked for
gb-298-what = goatbuilder federate rotate <partner> ipsec-leaf|ipsec-ca records the rotation it starts in <work>/.rotation-ipsec (its id, its kind and, for ipsec-ca, the partner's attestation: the fingerprint it read back of our new CA) before phase 11 runs, and removes the record once phase 11 passed. Each rotation is also kept in <work>/ipsec-rotations.json, with when it switched, passed phase 14 and was retired. A run that finds a record resumes that rotation with the same id, so goat-peering-edge sets nothing aside twice. This run was refused before anything ran: it asked for another kind or another read-back than the record names (fingerprints are compared in one form, sha256: and lowercase), the record is empty or not a rotation record, a retire was asked while a rotation is under way or of the other kind than the rotation retired, or a new rotation was asked while an earlier one switched and is not retired.
gb-298-cause-1 = an earlier rotation stopped before phase 11 passed, and a rotation of the other kind was asked for
gb-298-cause-2 = the partner's read-back changed between the run that started the rotation and this one
gb-298-cause-3 = an earlier rotation switched and its retire step has not run
gb-298-cause-4 = the record was emptied or edited by hand
gb-309-title = the retire step finds no confirmed rotation to retire
gb-309-what = The retire step of an IPsec credential rotation (goat-peering-edge, peering_edge_rotation_retire) removes the set-aside key in .previous-<id>/ on the edge, and nothing else: it never moves the live pair, the staged pair or our CA. It removes the key only once the new leaf is live: the confirm recorded it in .previous-<id>/confirmed (the unique id of the IKE SA the re-authentication made, and the time), the live pair is not the set-aside one, the live key is the live certificate's, and that SA, or one established after the confirm's time, has an installed CHILD SA. The confirm is recorded only once the SAs from before the re-authentication are gone, and a roll-back removes the record. This edge has no .previous-<id>/ for the id given, or the rotation is not confirmed live, so nothing was moved or removed.
gb-309-cause-1 = the id is mistyped, or names a rotation of another edge
gb-309-cause-2 = the rotation stopped before its switch, so the live pair is still the set-aside one
gb-309-cause-3 = the rotation was not confirmed: it stopped after the switch and before its confirm wrote .previous-<id>/confirmed
gb-309-cause-4 = the confirm's record is unreadable: it lacks the id= or at= line the confirm writes
gb-309-cause-5 = neither the IKE SA the confirm recorded nor one established since is up, or the live key is not the live certificate's
gb-309-cause-6 = goatbuilder federate rotate --retire: this side's ledger (<work>/ipsec-rotations.json) shows no such rotation whose phase 11 switched and whose phase 14 passed here after the switch, or it is already retired
gb-311-title = an IPsec rotation stopped half way through its switch, and what is left does not finish it
gb-311-what = The switch of an IPsec credential rotation (goat-peering-edge) moves the staged key, then the staged certificate, over the live names. A run killed between the two moves leaves one moved in and the other still staged. The next run finishes the remaining move when what is left is the moved one's match: the moved file is not the set-aside one, and the two halves are one key pair (each parsed by openssl; a file that does not parse matches nothing). Here they are not, so the live pair would be mixed: nothing was moved.
gb-311-cause-1 = the staged file left behind was replaced after the run stopped
gb-311-cause-2 = someone moved or edited the live or set-aside files by hand
gb-313-title = phase 8 refused the edge's certificate request for an edge leaf
gb-313-what = On a two-driver peering the edge makes its two leaf keys and sends only their certificate requests, which phase 8 signs with our CA (mint-peering-leaves.sh --request, peering_leaf_material.py). A request is signed only when its subject is exactly one attribute, CN = the name phase 8 derives for that leaf, it carries no extension or attribute, its key is EC P-256, it is signed with ECDSA and SHA-256 or stronger and its self-signature verifies, and its key is new: not the other request's, not our CA's, not another leaf's in the output folder or a previous set there, and not one the key record lists as signed before. The certificate's names come from the agreement, never from the request. One request failed, so neither edge leaf was signed and nothing was written.
gb-313-cause-1 = the request was made with another name, or with extra subject attributes, extensions or attributes
gb-313-cause-2 = the edge's key is not P-256, or the request is signed with SHA-1 or another weak algorithm
gb-313-cause-3 = the edge reused a key: the same key for both leaves, our CA's key, a controller leaf's key, or a key signed for this peering before (the key record outlives the output folder)
gb-313-cause-4 = the request file was changed on the way, or holds anything besides one request
gb-328-title = a rotation's values reach no play in the phases this run built
gb-328-what = A key rotation of `goatbuilder federate rotate` passes its values (the rotation id, and for an IPsec rotation its kind and read-back) to the plays of one phase: phase 11 for ipsec-leaf and ipsec-ca, phase 2 for the peering key. It puts them before each play's `-e fed_work=`. The phase this run built has no such play, so the values would reach nothing and the phase would run as a plain one and report the rotation done. Nothing ran. The phases are built from the agreement each run, and the rotation already checked that the agreement chose its transport, so this is goatbuilder's own fault, not the peering's.
gb-328-cause-1 = the phase's command in this goatbuilder no longer runs its play with `-e fed_work=`
gb-341-title = the IPsec rotation was not tested with this strongSwan
gb-341-what = An IPsec credential rotation (goat-peering-edge) relies on what strongSwan does when the switch loads the credentials: that swanctl --load-creds unloads the old leaf's key, and what --load-creds --clear and --list-certs show after it. That was measured on a real charon of the series in peering_edge_rotation_strongswan_tested (5.9 and 6.0 by default: 5.9.8 and 6.0.4). Before anything moves, the rotation reads the running charon's version (swanctl --version --daemon --raw). It reported a series not in the list, or no version at all, so nothing was moved.
gb-341-cause-1 = the edge runs a strongSwan release of another series than the rotation was measured on
gb-341-cause-2 = charon is not running, or swanctl cannot reach it over vici
gb-342-title = the IPsec rotation's new SA could not be shown to run on the new leaf; the old pair was put back
gb-342-what = swanctl --list-sas names only the identities, which an old and a new leaf share, so goat-peering-edge proves the leaf another way. Once the new IKE SA is up and the SAs from before the re-authentication are gone, it reloads the credentials from the files alone (swanctl --load-creds --clear) and reads swanctl --list-certs. charon signs only with a private key it holds, and the partner checks the signature against the certificate we send, so the new SA ran on the live leaf when charon lists exactly one end-entity certificate of our id (peering_edge_local_id): the live leaf's serial, holding its private key, on a key other than the set-aside leaf's. Here it did not: charon lists another certificate of our id, does not hold the live leaf with its key, or the live leaf has the set-aside leaf's key. The message says which and names the serials. The old pair was put back and loaded, the old leaf is live, nothing was recorded, and the rotation is marked rolled back, so every later run of its id reports GB-302.
gb-342-cause-1 = another certificate and key of our id are still in the swanctl directory under other file names
gb-342-cause-2 = charon did not load the new pair
gb-342-cause-3 = the live leaf was re-certified on the old key, and a resume found it switched in
gb-343-title = another peering's credentials on the edge are missing, so the IPsec rotation does not reload them
gb-343-what = Once the new IKE SA is up, an IPsec credential rotation (goat-peering-edge) reloads every credential in the edge's swanctl directory from the files alone (swanctl --load-creds --clear), which unloads any key whose file is gone. So before anything moves, and again just before that reload, it checks every other peering on the edge (each conf.d/<name>.conf the role wrote): its certs file in x509/ is present and readable, a readable key in private/ is that certificate's key, and its cacerts files in x509ca/ are present and readable. One was not; the message names the peering and the file. Before anything moves, nothing was moved. Just before the reload, the reload was not run, the old pair was put back and the rotation is marked rolled back.
gb-343-cause-1 = another peering's certificate, key or CA file was removed or made unreadable
gb-343-cause-2 = a peering was removed by hand, leaving its conf.d file behind
gb-351-title = a Spock subscription has forward_origins set, which in a mesh replicates in a loop
gb-351-what = A Spock subscription, or the setting that creates them, has forward_origins other than empty or 'none'. In a mesh of three or more sites such a subscription passes each change on to the next site, which passes it back, without end, while every subscription still reads replicating and nothing is logged. The spock-forward-origins-check role stopped the run before spock-mesh-peering or ory-narrow-repset changed anything. The message names the setting, or each subscription as <host> <database>.<subscription>.
gb-351-cause-1 = a site's group_vars set spock_mesh_forward_origins or ory_spock_forward_origins
gb-351-cause-2 = a subscription was created by hand with forward_origins := '{"{"}all{"}"}'
gb-352-title = a joining site cannot take a shared secret from its founder
gb-352-what = join-node's phase 3 gives a joining site the values every site of the goatnet shares (the SPEC lines of newsite-secrets.sh marked cluster: NetBird's datastore key, the replicator, the relay secret, Ory's network id, the Kratos cipher, the Hydra system secret) by taking its founder's. Under a store of references the founder's name has no reference in .envrc.local.d/*.refs; under a store of values it has no value; or, after the secrets injector ran, the founder's or the joiner's name was empty or still a reference. Nothing was written for the joiner's shared names. The message names the variables, never a value.
gb-352-cause-1 = the founder's value was never stored under the declared secrets posture on this machine
gb-352-cause-2 = the store's tool is not signed in, so with-secrets could not resolve the reference
gb-352-cause-3 = join_founder_site in the joiner's group_vars names the wrong site
gb-353-title = a joining site already holds a shared secret that is not its founder's
gb-353-what = join-node's phase 3 takes a joining site's shared values from its founder and never overwrites one the joiner already holds. A joiner name holds a value (or a reference to one) whose SHA-256 differs from the founder's. A joiner with a fresh value looks healthy and cannot take part: NetBird cannot decrypt the replicated rows, Spock does not replicate, Kratos and Hydra cannot read the replicated identity data. The value was kept. The message names the variables, never a value or a digest.
gb-353-cause-1 = the joiner's values were minted fresh, by hand or by newsite-secrets.sh, before this step existed
gb-353-cause-2 = the joiner's reference points at another goatnet's entry
gb-354-title = the env store holds none of a joining site's shared secrets
gb-354-what = join-node's phase 3 takes a joining site's shared values from its founder. Under the env store nothing persists, so it cannot write them: each joiner name must already be exported in the shell that runs join-node. One or more are not. Nothing was written.
gb-354-cause-1 = the declared store is env (GOAT_SECRET_STORE=env or secrets.store: env)
gb-355-title = a standup play limited to the founder selects no host of the site's group
gb-355-what = Six standup plays run on the site's first management host alone (SPIRE, the LAN service surface, the LAN TLS routes, the dashboard, the portal, grant-keyd), limited with -l 'mgmt-<site>-01*', the declared naming rule. Before the play, the same command checked the site's group, <site>_hosts, in inventory/mgmt-hosts.ini and inventory/mgmt-hosts-mesh.ini, and no host there matches the limit. The play was refused before ansible ran: with another host matching the pattern, ansible prints "skipping: no hosts matched" and exits 0 having done nothing. Both avenues, goatbuilder standup and goat-standup.sh, run the check (ops/scripts/inventory-hosts.py --match --require).
gb-355-cause-1 = the site's first management host is named otherwise than mgmt-<site>-01 in the inventory
gb-355-cause-2 = the host is listed under another group than <site>_hosts
gb-357-title = a member deviation no longer names the value its goatnet resolves to
gb-357-what = A member host-group's deviation in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml carries `over:`, the value its goatnet resolved the component to when the deviation was written. The goatnet now resolves it to something else: a goatnet deviation was added or changed (a hotfix), or fleet moved (a graduation, a fastlane). The member deviation still wins by precedence, so that member would keep its old pin and silently miss the change. Nothing was rendered.
gb-357-cause-1 = a hotfix or other goatnet deviation was added after the member deviation was written
gb-357-cause-2 = fleet moved, by graduation or fastlane, and the member deviation was not looked at
gb-358-title = a member deviation does not name the value it overrides, or `over:` is where it means nothing
gb-358-what = Every member host-group deviation with a `value:` in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml names, in `over:`, what its goatnet resolves the component to without it: a ref, `absent`, or `none` (fleet null and no goatnet deviation). This one has no `over:`, or `over:` sits on a goatnet deviation or an `absent: true` one, where it means nothing. The message prints the line to paste. Nothing was rendered.
gb-358-cause-1 = the table predates ADR 1256, so its member deviations carry no `over:`
gb-358-cause-2 = `over:` was added to a goatnet-scope or an absent member deviation
gb-359-title = federate allocate names a peering the config does not declare
gb-359-what = `goatbuilder federate allocate <peering>` allocates the ports, addresses and names of one declared peering. The peering named on the command line is not in the config's `peerings:`, and a peering nobody declared is one nobody reviewed (contract C1). Nothing was allocated or written. A peering run whose partner is not declared is GB-149.
gb-359-cause-1 = the peering name was mistyped
gb-359-cause-2 = the peering is declared in another environment's config, or not yet declared at all
gb-360-title = a machine's record was not read
gb-360-what = The converger asked one machine for its newest record of a component, and the machine was not read: it did not answer, the inventory names it and it was silent, or its read FAILED. Contract C7: such a machine is unknown, never done, and nothing about its record is assumed.
gb-360-cause-1 = the host is down, or not reachable from this machine over the transport the environment uses
gb-360-cause-2 = the inventory names a host that gave no answer
gb-360-cause-3 = the read ran on the host and FAILED (become refused, no python)
gb-362-title = a site's own copy of a generic play does not say it was made from the current one
gb-362-what = A deployment may keep its own ops/ansible/<site>-<suffix> (for example <site>-seed-from-peer.yml), which the drivers run in place of the generic site-<suffix>. A copy names the version of the generic play it was made from in a first line `# derived-from: site-<suffix> sha256:<hex>`. This copy has no such line, or names a digest the current generic play no longer has, so the fixes the generic play gained since cannot be known to be in it. Nothing ran on the hosts; the run stopped before the site's play.
gb-362-cause-1 = the copy predates the derived-from line
gb-362-cause-2 = the generic play changed after the copy was made (a fix, a new guard)
gb-362-cause-3 = the copy was edited from another checkout's generic play
gb-365-title = a pins file breaks the pins schema
gb-365-what = goatbuilder pins, and every verb that runs its check (release, preflight, the fast lane), reads ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml, its sidecar ops/pins/lite-pins.yaml, ops/pins/parity-capabilities.yaml and the unmigrated-pins list ops/pins/unmigrated-pins.txt, and holds them, and each intent it renders, to the pins schema: the shape of each table, a multi-arch index digest on every image, one owner for each member and var, a reason on every deviation. One rule is broken, and the message names the file, the component or goatnet, and the rule. Nothing was rendered.
gb-365-cause-1 = a table or component is not in the shape the schema gives it (a list where a mapping goes, a key the sidecar does not carry)
gb-365-cause-2 = an image is pinned by a mutable or bare tag, not an index digest
gb-365-cause-3 = a goatnet, member or var is declared twice, or names a host group that does not exist
gb-365-cause-4 = a deviation has no reason, or sets both absent: and value:
gb-365-cause-5 = a row of the unmigrated-pins list is not <member_group>:<var>
gb-365-cause-6 = a release pin names another repository than its release.signer, or a release file name holds a character other than A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ + -
gb-368-title = an environment value is still an unresolved secret reference
gb-368-what = ops/scripts/ansible-preflight.sh found a variable whose value still begins with op://, bao:// or bw:// after the secrets injector ran. A play renders the environment as it finds it, so the reference would become a container's literal password or cookie secret. The message names each variable, never its value. Nothing was run on any host.
gb-368-cause-1 = the command was not run through ops/scripts/with-secrets, so nothing resolved the references
gb-368-cause-2 = an inherited GOAT_SECRETS_RESOLVED told the injector the environment was already resolved
gb-368-cause-3 = GOAT_SECRET_REF_PREFIX names one store's scheme while a reference of another scheme is in the environment
gb-369-title = a phase's command reads an environment variable that is not set
gb-369-what = A phase's command expands $NAME or ${"{"}NAME{"}"}, and nothing set NAME in the environment goatbuilder runs it in. Phases run under bash -u, so the command would stop with 'NAME: unbound variable' before its program starts, partway through the node. join-node checks every phase it will run before the first one, and ops/scripts/standup-phase-run checks each standup phase after loading the secrets store, and each refuses, naming the variables; a phase that stopped this way prints bash's line, which this code names.
gb-369-cause-1 = the variable is kept under the secrets posture, which loads it for the commands it wraps, not for the shell that expands the phase's command (a founder's OWNER_EMAIL, the bootstrap administrator's address)
gb-369-cause-2 = the variable was exported in another shell, or before a restart
gb-361-title = a joining site's data router and health publishers would name its Zenoh stack differently
gb-361-what = join-node deploys a joining site's data router (the goat-zenoh role) and then each health publisher the site declares. `zenoh_stack` names the router's directories, its container (`<stack>-router`) and its network (`<stack>_default`). The router's role defaults it to `<site_id>-zenoh`; a publisher's role reads it with a default of its own (`goat-zenoh`) or requires it. Ansible gives each role only its own defaults, so with no `zenoh_stack` in the site's group_vars the publishers would look for a router and network the site's router does not have. The message names each publisher and the value it would read. The plan was refused before any phase ran.
gb-361-cause-1 = the site's group_vars do not set zenoh_stack, and a publisher role's default differs from the router role's
gb-364-title = a health publisher join-node deploys has no leaf in the site's leaf manifest
gb-364-what = join-node deploys each health publisher the end-state model marks present at a joining site, and each one runs with its own leaf certificate, which phase 13 mints from the site's leaf manifest (ops/enrollment/manifests/<site>-certs.yaml) and stages onto the node. The model marks a publisher present whose leaf the manifest does not carry, so the plan was refused before any phase ran, rather than at staging or at deploy.
gb-364-cause-1 = the manifest was written before the publisher was declared at the site
gb-364-cause-2 = the pin table declares a publisher at the site that the site does not mean to run
gb-367-title = a joining site's seed tables differ from its founder's around the Spock wiring
gb-367-what = join-node seeds a joining site from its founder (site-seed-from-peer.yml, data only) and then wires Spock with synchronize_data false, so the subscriptions carry only writes made after they exist. A founder write made after the seed and before the subscription from the founder existed is never sent. spock-seed-current-check.yml compares every seed table on the founder and on the joiner, by row count and a checksum of its rows: before pass 3 wires Spock, and again after the joiner's Spock wiring, once each subscription from the founder has confirmed the founder's current position. A table that differs, or a subscription that does not catch up in time, stops phase 13. The message names each table and its two row counts, never a row.
gb-367-cause-1 = the founder took writes (a sign-in, a new identity, a peer, a setup key) between the seed and the Spock wiring, for example when phase 13 was resumed long after pass 2
gb-367-cause-2 = a subscription from the founder is not replicating, so it cannot catch up
gb-370-title = no usable peering directory URL or id was given
gb-370-what = goatbuilder federate directory needs the directory's base URL, from --url or FED_DIRECTORY_URL, as an http(s) URL; and a party or counterparty id as the directory accepts it, 2 to 16 of a-z, 0-9 and -. Nothing was requested.
gb-370-cause-1 = neither --url nor FED_DIRECTORY_URL is set in this shell
gb-370-cause-2 = the URL has no https:// in front of it
gb-370-cause-3 = the id was typed with capitals, a slash or a dot
gb-371-title = the peering directory did not answer, or what answered is not a peering directory
gb-371-what = goatbuilder federate directory asked the directory for /v1/version (and, on a release older than saltlick v0.6.0, /healthz) and got no answer, or an answer that is not a saltlick directory.
gb-371-cause-1 = the URL is wrong, or names a host that is not the directory
gb-371-cause-2 = the directory is down or restarting (a deploy replaces its instance, a few minutes)
gb-371-cause-3 = this machine has no route to the internet, or a proxy refuses the host
gb-372-title = the peering directory lacks a file a peering reads
gb-372-what = goatbuilder federate directory check (or show) found an item that is not there. Each missing item is one of two kinds, and the view names which. NOT HOSTED: the directory serves the route, and the party has not uploaded the file. ROUTE MISSING: the directory's release does not serve the route at all (a plain-text 404, or a route absent from its /v1/version list).
gb-372-cause-1 = NOT HOSTED: the party has not run phase 5 of the peering, which uploads its entry, CA, SPIFFE bundle and its package for the counterparty
gb-372-cause-2 = NOT HOSTED: the party used another id, or uploaded to the single package slot instead of the one for this counterparty
gb-372-cause-3 = ROUTE MISSING: the directory runs a release older than the one that added the route (per-counterparty packages need saltlick v0.5.0)
gb-373-title = the ref is not on the rung below yet
gb-373-what = A deploy to a goatnet above the lowest rung of the release ladder (ops/release/ladder.yaml: dev, then preprod, then prod) was refused before any host was touched. The digest it would deploy is not what the rung below pins, or the rung below has not converged on it, so the change has not run anywhere first (ADR 1120). deploy-component.sh checks the pin and reads the lower rung's deploy ledgers; ansible-preflight.sh checks the pin for a direct play, including an -e override of the image variable (ops/release/ladder_gate.py).
gb-373-cause-1 = the pin moved on this goatnet before it moved on the rung below
gb-373-cause-2 = an -e override deployed a ref the rung below does not run
gb-373-cause-3 = the rung below pins the ref, but its hosts never converged on it (their ledgers name another ref)
gb-373-cause-4 = no rung below runs the component at all
gb-374-title = a machine type or platform family file is not valid
gb-374-what = join-node and standup read what a machine converges as from ops/goatbuilder/targets/ and ops/goatbuilder/families/, and a private overlay's own files from ops/goatbuilder/overlay/. One of them is not readable, has an unknown field, names a value or a built-in step this goatbuilder does not have, runs a play its words do not limit to the type's group, or repeats a name. Or a host_vars or group_vars file that may declare a machine's platform_family is not readable YAML, or declares it as other than one word. The message names the file and why. Nothing ran.
gb-374-cause-1 = a misspelt or unknown field in the file
gb-374-cause-2 = an overlay file with the name of a type or family the repository already has
gb-374-cause-3 = the file was written for another goatbuilder release: a main checkout newer than this goatbuilder release can change a file's format (goatbuilder --version names the release)
gb-374-cause-4 = a play's args set target more than once, to another group, or with -l
gb-374-cause-5 = a vars file with a YAML error, or platform_family as a list or mapping
gb-375-title = the platform family has no file
gb-375-what = A type file accepts, or a machine declares (platform_family), a platform family that has no file in ops/goatbuilder/families/ or ops/goatbuilder/overlay/families/. The message lists the families there are. Nothing ran.
gb-375-cause-1 = a misspelt family
gb-375-cause-2 = a model named where a family belongs (a type and a machine name families)
gb-375-cause-3 = the overlay that has the family's file is not in this tree
gb-376-title = the machine's platform family is not one its type accepts
gb-376-what = The machine declares its platform family (platform_family in host_vars, its inventory line or its group's group_vars), and the type it is to become accepts other families. The message names both, and the file the declaration is in. Nothing ran.
gb-376-cause-1 = the role was given to the wrong machine
gb-376-cause-2 = platform_family is wrong for this machine
gb-377-title = a play the machine type converges with is not in this tree
gb-377-what = A type file may name a play of another repository or a private overlay, so a public clone reads and plans every type. A run needs the play. The message names it and where it comes from. Nothing ran on the machine.
gb-377-cause-1 = the run is from a public clone, and the play is in an overlay or another repository
gb-377-cause-2 = the tree was assembled without the repository that has the play
gb-378-title = a machine type or platform family file is not goatcore's
gb-378-what = In an assembled tree (.goat-assembled at its root) the files in ops/goatbuilder/targets/ and ops/goatbuilder/families/ are held to the goatcore commit the marker names. One differs from that commit, is not in it, or cannot be compared with it. An overlay adds types and families, in ops/goatbuilder/overlay/, and replaces none. Nothing ran.
gb-378-cause-1 = the overlay carries a file of a built-in type or family
gb-378-cause-2 = the overlay's own type is in ops/goatbuilder/targets/
gb-378-cause-3 = a file was edited in the assembled tree
gb-378-cause-4 = the tree's git does not have the goatcore commit, or the tree was assembled from local directories
gb-379-title = not built for a release component
gb-379-what = The component is artifact_type release in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml: it ships as a file, not an image (gate J23). Deploy, verify and rollback to the file before work on one. What was asked does not: a host keeps the one file before the one in use, so a rollback is one step; and a rollback of one cannot be saved as a plan. Nothing ran.
gb-379-cause-1 = rollback was asked for more than one step
gb-379-cause-2 = `goatbuilder plan rollback` was asked for a release component
gb-380-title = a network tap's mode is not declared, or its declaration is not valid
gb-380-what = A machine of a type that declares `tap` (the network tap) states in its host_vars how it sits on the link it records: `tap:` with `mode` (listen, which fails open, or inline, which fails closed: the link is down whenever the machine is), `source` when listening (copper-tap, bypass-relay or mirror-port), `ports` and `management`. There is no default. The machine declares none, or the message names what in its declaration is wrong and the file it is in. Nothing ran.
gb-380-cause-1 = the machine's host_vars have no `tap:`
gb-380-cause-2 = a key is missing, unknown or templated, or the number of ports does not fit the mode and source
gb-380-cause-3 = the management interface is also listed as a port
gb-381-title = a release component's file is not fetched or does not verify
gb-381-what = A release component (fleet-pins `artifact_type: release`) is fetched and verified on the operator's machine before any host is touched: the release's checksums file must have the pin's SHA-256 digest; its keyless signature beside it (`<checksums>.cosign-bundle`, or `<checksums>.sigstore.json` when the component says `release.bundle: sigstore`) must verify for the component's `release.signer` at the pin's tag, or, with `release.bundle: attestation`, each file's own GitHub attestation must; and each architecture's file must be listed in the checksums file with the SHA-256 it has. The pin's repository must also be the repository of the signer's workflow. The message names the check that refused (`repository`, `pin`, `declaration`, `trust-root`, `fetch`, `digest`, `signature`, `arch`, `listed`, `store`, `play`, or `goatbuilder` when deploy-component.sh finds no goatbuilder that can verify). No host was touched. `verify` prints it when it could not fetch the release to compare a host's file with; it reads hosts and changes none.
gb-381-cause-1 = the release was changed after it was pinned, or the pin's digest is wrong
gb-381-cause-2 = the release was signed by another workflow, repository or tag than the component declares
gb-381-cause-3 = the pin names another repository than the one whose workflow is release.signer
gb-381-cause-4 = the release has no file for this architecture, or its checksums file does not list it
gb-381-cause-5 = the play its archive carries (`release.play`) is not there, is not the archive's on disk, or would add what is not its own: variables, a role goatcore has, Ansible code of its own, a path outside its directory; or one of its plays runs on other hosts than the target group
gb-381-cause-6 = the release or its signature cannot be downloaded from here, and no earlier run kept it
gb-381-cause-7 = ops/scripts/deploy-component.sh found no goatbuilder, or one older than this check (GOATBUILDER_BIN names one)
gb-382-title = a pin names no digest
gb-382-what = A member group's pins.yml declares the component's ref with no @sha256: digest: the value as YAML reads it (a trailing comment is not part of it) must end in @sha256: and exactly 64 lowercase hex characters. A tag can be moved to another image, so deploy installs only a ref pinned by its digest (contract C1). goatbuilder deploy and deploy-component.sh refuse it before any host, whatever the fresh-source check said of the tree; a dry run reports it, prints the whole plan and exits 1. --verify-only deploys nothing and is not judged.
gb-382-cause-1 = the digest was removed from group_vars/<group>/pins.yml by hand
gb-382-cause-2 = the value in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml was written as a tag, and pins.yml was rendered from it
gb-383-title = the goatnet's signed intent does not verify
gb-383-what = Before any host, deploy (goatbuilder and deploy-component.sh) verifies the goatnet's signed intent from the checkout alone, with the checks `goatbuilder pins --check` makes of it: ops/consistency/intent/<goatnet>/intent.json is there, its content_hash is its body's, its detached signature verifies against the committed public key of the CA it names, it is what the tree renders, and each member group's pins.yml is what the pins table renders. One does not hold, or could not be judged (openssl missing, a file that does not parse): the run is refused, and the line says which. A dry run reports it, prints the whole plan and exits 1. --verify-only deploys nothing and is not judged.
gb-383-cause-1 = a pin was changed and the intent was not regenerated and signed again
gb-383-cause-2 = pins.yml or intent.json was edited by hand
gb-383-cause-3 = the goatnet's intent was never signed, or its CA's public key is not in ops/enrollment/public-keys/
gb-383-cause-4 = openssl, or for deploy-component.sh the Python module jsonschema, is not on this machine
gb-384-title = a target type lists a service its machine cannot have
gb-384-what = A target type's `services:` names components of ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml, and its machine's build deploys each one. The message names the service and what is missing: the component, the machine's group among the goatnet's members, the pin rendered for that group, or the component's deploy play. A list is a requirement, so it is refused and never skipped; a component the pins declare `absent: true` for the group is skipped. Nothing ran.
gb-384-cause-1 = the type lists a name that is not a component
gb-384-cause-2 = the machine's group is not a member group of the goatnet, or goatbuilder pins has not been run since
gb-384-cause-3 = the component's play is in another repository, and this tree was not assembled with it
gb-385-title = a component's play was not taken from its source repository
gb-385-what = The component's play is in another repository (`source:` in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml: repository, a full commit or a tag with the SSH key that signs it, play). It is fetched on the operator's machine with that person's own git access before any host is touched. The message names the check that refused: `fetch` (not reachable with this access, or no such commit or tag), `tag` (a lightweight tag, another tag under its name, or one made before the tag an earlier run took), `signer` (the tag carries no SSH signature, or not the declared key's), `commit` (the copy kept here is not exactly that commit), `play` (the commit has no such file), `role` (it carries a role goatcore has), `tree` (it carries variables, modules, plugins or collections of its own, a link, a submodule, or a file where a role of goatcore's reads one beside the playbook), `declaration`, or `goatbuilder` when deploy-component.sh finds no goatbuilder that can fetch it. No host was touched.
gb-385-cause-1 = this person's git access does not reach the repository
gb-385-cause-2 = the commit was never pushed, or was removed
gb-385-cause-3 = a file in the kept copy was changed
gb-415-title = first-bite's capture is not declared, or the dictionary it names is not in the release
gb-415-what = A machine of the type first-bite declares, in its host_vars, `first_bite_interface`, the interface first-bite captures on, and may name in `first_bite_dictionary` one of the dictionaries the release carries (the directory names under `dictionaries/` in its archive). There is no default. The machine declares no interface, or one that is not an interface name, or a dictionary that is not a directory name: ops/ansible/first-bite-deploy.yml stops before anything, and nothing was installed. Or the release has no dictionary of that name: the verified archive is already in place and its ledger row written, the play stops before first-bite's installer runs, a first-bite installed before keeps running as it was, and the play removes the component's `current` link, so verify reports the machine not converged.
gb-415-cause-1 = the machine's host_vars have no `first_bite_interface`
gb-415-cause-2 = the interface or dictionary is not a plain name
gb-415-cause-3 = the release has no dictionary of that name
gb-416-title = first-bite's settings capture on another interface than the machine declares, or do not decode with the installed dictionary
gb-416-what = first-bite's installer keeps a settings file it finds (/etc/first-bite/first-bite.env), so a machine whose declared `first_bite_interface` changed still captures on the interface that file names, and a machine first installed with no dictionary has no `BITE_DICT_PATH` when one is declared later. ops/ansible/first-bite-deploy.yml reads the file after the installer ran and stops when it names another interface, or when a dictionary is declared and the file does not point at the installed one. The play removed the component's `current` link, so verify reports the machine not converged.
gb-416-cause-1 = the machine's `first_bite_interface` changed after first-bite was installed
gb-416-cause-2 = the settings file was edited by hand
gb-416-cause-3 = the machine declared a dictionary after first-bite was installed without one
gb-417-title = a joining node or its founder does not declare the replication phase 13 wires
gb-417-what = join-node phase 13 at a joining site seeds the core node from its founder, then wires Spock on both sides: the node's own bootstrap, and spock-mesh-wire.yml on the founder. Those plays run their Spock roles only on a host whose own variables declare participates_in_spock true, an inter_site_spock_topology other than none and, for the kratos and hydra databases, ory_spock_enabled true (on router_role A); on any other host they do nothing and report done, so the founder gets no Ory replication node for the node to subscribe to. A goatlite founder declares none of them. The NetBird management tables replicate only where `spock_mesh_databases` names the netbird database pulling the `netbird_mgmt` replication set, whose repset holds the curated table list (ADR 1084 D1); the seed copies exactly that list and both seed-current checks compare it, so a site without it would be seeded once and then drift. Before phase 13 changes anything, spock-join-replication-declared.yml reads those variables on the node and on the founder's A host, as Ansible evaluates them, and stops naming each host, each missing value and the group_vars it belongs in. Nothing was changed.
gb-417-cause-1 = the founder began as a goatlite, which renders participates_in_spock false and no ory_spock_enabled
gb-417-cause-2 = the joining site's group_vars were copied from a founder that never replicated
gb-417-cause-3 = the run's inventory holds no host of the founder's group with router_role A
gb-417-cause-4 = a site declares no `spock_mesh_databases`, or its netbird entry pulls another set, or its `netbird_mgmt` repset holds other tables than the seed's
gb-425-title = no model of a family the type accepts delivers the encryption it requires
gb-425-what = A type file requires an encryption scope (`encryption: root`), and it accepts a platform family none of whose models' entries delivers that scope (each model's `encryption.scope` in ops/goatbuilder/families/<family>.yaml). A model that is only listed, or whose scope is unknown, delivers nothing. The message names the family and each model's scope. Nothing ran.
gb-425-cause-1 = the type accepts a family whose models encrypt a data volume, not the root
gb-425-cause-2 = the family lists its models' names with no entry for each
gb-425-cause-3 = a model's entry has the wrong scope
gb-410-title = what a small computer's model delivers cannot be read
gb-410-what = `doctor --target <type> --live` holds each machine that declares the small-computer family to what its model delivers: the model's entry in ops/goatbuilder/families/small-computer.yaml (its arch, os.ubuntu, encryption with its unlock list, and its end_state: board, root_media and fips). The family has no such file, lists its models' names with no entry for each, has more than one model and which one the machine is is not declared (not built), or the model's entry has no end_state, an encrypted scope other than root, or an architecture the check does not know. The machine was not read, and its rows are unknown.
gb-410-cause-1 = a model was added to ops/goatbuilder/families/small-computer.yaml with no end_state
gb-410-cause-2 = the family's file was changed by hand
gb-411-title = goatbuilder image cannot build this model, or the builder has no such step
gb-411-what = `goatbuilder image <model>` runs a model's golden path when that model's builder is one goatbuilder wraps: today `ops/rpi-base-image/` for `rpi5`, read from the model's entry in its family's file under ops/goatbuilder/families/: its `builder` and its `os.golden_image` (file, sha256, url). The model is not there, its builder is another (a Karbon's or a Jetson's script, which goatbuilder does not run yet), its golden image entry is missing or malformed, or `--step` names a step the builder does not have. Nothing ran.
gb-411-cause-1 = the model's name is misspelt, or it is a model of another builder
gb-411-cause-2 = the model's golden_image entry was removed or changed by hand
gb-411-cause-3 = --step names a step of another unlock or console (yubikey is not a step with --unlock passphrase; headless-unlock only with --headless)
gb-412-title = an image step did not run, because what it stands on is not there
gb-412-what = Each step of `goatbuilder image <model> --step <name>` runs only when the step before it is proven, and when it has what it needs: the card (`--device`) for flashing or reading it back, the machine (`--host`, as ssh reaches it) for a step that runs on it, and on the machine the builder's own copy of the step's script, byte for byte this checkout's (the card carries it from the flash). The message names what is missing, the step whose proof does not hold and how to read it, or the script that differs. Nothing ran.
gb-412-cause-1 = --device or --host was left out
gb-412-cause-2 = the step before was not run, or its proof does not hold yet (a reboot not made, an unlock not done)
gb-412-cause-3 = the card was flashed from another checkout, or the script was edited on the Pi
gb-413-title = an image step ran and failed, or its proof does not hold
gb-413-what = `goatbuilder image <model> --step <name>` ran the builder's script for the step and the script failed (its own words, above, say why), or it finished and the step's proof, read straight after, does not hold: the message names each row that does not. The machine is as the script left it; the builder's scripts are each re-runnable.
gb-413-cause-1 = the script stopped on something it checks (its words above)
gb-413-cause-2 = the script finished and the machine is not yet in the state it set up (a reboot or an unlock still to come)
gb-395-title = the goat-processors runner's configuration is not supplied
gb-395-what = ops/ansible/goat-processors-runner-deploy.yml installs the goat-processors runner (a release component, gate J24) by the runner's deploy contract, and the runner's whole configuration is the goatnet's: goatcore holds no default. A member group that runs it sets goat_processors_runner_config, the whole runner.yaml as a mapping, and goat_processors_runner_tls ({"{"}ca, certificate, private_key{"}"}, PEM text) when its fabric uses tls/ locators. One is missing, empty or not a mapping, so the play stopped at its first task. Nothing was installed.
gb-395-cause-1 = the member group's group_vars do not set goat_processors_runner_config
gb-395-cause-2 = it is set to something that is not a mapping, or to an empty one
gb-395-cause-3 = goat_processors_runner_tls is set, and is not a mapping
gb-420-title = a target type's plays were not taken from its source repository
gb-420-what = The type's plays marked `from: source` are in another repository (`source:` in the type file: repository, and a full commit or a tag with the SSH key that signs it). A run of join-node or standup fetches that commit on the operator's machine with that person's own git access, into the state directory, before its first phase; no phase has run when it is refused there. The message names the check that refused: `fetch` (not reachable with this access, or no such commit or tag), `tag` (a lightweight tag, another tag under its name, or one made before the tag an earlier run took), `signer` (the tag carries no SSH signature, or not the declared key's), `commit` (the copy kept here is not exactly that commit), `play` (the commit has no such file), `role` (it carries a role goatcore has), `tree` (it carries variables, modules, plugins or collections of its own, a link, a submodule, or a file where a role of goatcore's reads one beside the playbook), or `declaration`. A tag is fetched and checked on every run. The same checks run again just before each of its plays; a refusal there stops that phase before the play. A plan fetches nothing.
gb-420-cause-1 = this person's git access does not reach the repository
gb-420-cause-2 = the commit was never pushed, or was removed
gb-420-cause-3 = a file in the kept copy was changed
gb-396-title = goat-xf-pipe-zenoh's conversion is not supplied
gb-396-what = ops/ansible/goat-xf-pipe-deploy.yml installs goat-xf-pipe-zenoh (a release component, gate J24) and runs it as a systemd service, and what it converts is the goatnet's: goatcore holds no default. A member group that runs it sets goat_xf_pipe with from_format and to_format (pack names), in, out and metrics (a loopback or mesh <address>:<port> of its Prometheus listener), and zenoh_config (JSON5 text) when its Zenoh URIs name ?config=. One is missing, empty, not text, holds a control character, a format is not a pack name, or metrics is not such an address, so the play stopped at its first task. Nothing was installed.
gb-396-cause-1 = the member group's group_vars do not set goat_xf_pipe
gb-396-cause-2 = one of from_format, to_format, in, out or metrics is missing or empty
gb-396-cause-3 = metrics is not a loopback (127.x, [::1]) or mesh (RFC 6598 shared address space) <address>:<port>
gb-396-cause-4 = a value holds a control character, a format is not a pack name, or zenoh_config is empty
gb-414-title = a machine lacks a value its type needs from the site
gb-414-what = A type file names `inputs`: values of the site's configuration a machine of the type needs, each an Ansible variable read where Ansible would for that machine (its host_vars, its inventory line, its group's group_vars, group_vars/all, the [group:vars] sections). The machine has the variable unset, or set to the role's placeholder (for network-jump, herd_connector_image is PENDING until an image is supplied). join-node refuses before any phase, a dry run too; standup refuses before any phase of a run that reaches phase 14; doctor --target shows the row. Nothing on the machine was changed.
gb-414-cause-1 = the variable was never set for the site
gb-414-cause-2 = it is set to the role's placeholder
gb-421-title = a service the type lists needs an acknowledgement this run was not given
gb-421-what = The machine's type lists a service (`services:`) whose rollout is not cleanly reversible: the component's rollback is declared `none` or `restore-required`, or does not fit its artifact type (ADR 1211). Its deploy proceeds only with a name that accepts that risk, which the deploy ledger row records. join-node and standup pass `--ack` to that deploy, and a run given none is refused before its first phase, rather than at the service's phase after the machine has changed. A dry run plans it and says so. No phase has run.
gb-421-cause-1 = the run was started without --ack
gb-345-title = there is nothing to seed again
gb-345-what = `goatbuilder join-node ... --reseed` copies a node's replicated tables again from its founder after the founder invalidated the slot it keeps for the node. Its first step (`spock-reseed.py lost`) read every slot the founder keeps for the node's subscriptions, and none reads `wal_status = lost`. A node whose slots are healthy catches up by itself; copying it again only costs the copy. Nothing was changed.
gb-345-cause-1 = the node came back before the founder's slot for it passed `max_slot_wal_keep_size`
gb-345-cause-2 = the node or the site named is not the one whose slot was lost
gb-345-cause-3 = a later step ran before the lost step passed in this journal
gb-346-title = the founder has not taken the node's own writes
gb-346-what = Before the re-seed truncates the node's tables, the founder must hold everything the node wrote while it was away. The founder's subscription from the node does not read `replicating`, or its slot on the node has not confirmed the node's current WAL position within the wait (`--wait`, 600 seconds by default). Truncating now would lose those writes, so nothing was changed. When the founder's slot on the node is lost, the step passes instead: the founder can never take those writes, and the export step carries them.
gb-346-cause-1 = the founder's subscription from the node is down or disabled
gb-346-cause-2 = the node's writes are still on their way: a large backlog, or a slow link
gb-346-cause-3 = the founder has no subscription from the node
gb-347-title = the copy is not well under the node's own slot cap
gb-347-what = The re-seed's copy is written as WAL on the node, and that WAL counts against the slot the node keeps for the founder. The replicated tables to copy hold more than the allowed share (`--size-fraction`, 0.25 by default) of the node's `max_slot_wal_keep_size`. A copy near the cap invalidates the founder's slot on the node, and the node's next writes could not reach the founder. Nothing was changed.
gb-347-cause-1 = the replicated tables are larger than the node's cap allows for
gb-347-cause-2 = the node runs with a small max_slot_wal_keep_size
gb-348-title = the copy did not finish
gb-348-what = After the re-seed dropped and created the node's subscription from the founder and asked for each table to be copied again, a table was still not copied (`spock.local_sync_status` other than `r`) or the subscription did not read `replicating` within the wait, or a slot on the node read `lost` during the copy. The node's tables may be partly copied; the founder's are untouched.
gb-348-cause-1 = the copy is still running: a large table, or a slow link
gb-348-cause-2 = a table failed to copy (the node's Postgres log says why)
gb-348-cause-3 = the copy cost the founder its slot on the node (GB-347's case)
gb-349-title = a table differs after the copy
gb-349-what = The re-seed's last step compares each copied table on the node and the founder by row count and digest, and one still differs after the wait. The node and the founder do not hold the same rows.
gb-349-cause-1 = writes still arriving on one side: a busy table converges a little later
gb-349-cause-2 = a write the copy did not carry, or a conflict resolved differently on each side
gb-350-title = the re-seed cannot name the Postgres container
gb-350-what = The re-seed reaches the node and the founder through the Postgres container the replication role names (`spock_mesh_postgres_container`). Neither the site's group_vars nor `ops/ansible/roles/spock-mesh-peering/defaults/main.yml` in this checkout sets it. Nothing ran.
gb-350-cause-1 = the checkout does not carry the spock-mesh-peering role's defaults
gb-387-title = a Spock slot is lost and can never be read again
gb-387-what = `goatbuilder join-node ... --slots` (the detector, `spock-reseed.py slots`) read every Spock subscription and slot of the node, in every database with a Spock node, and at least one slot reads `wal_status = lost` (or `conflicting`): Postgres removed WAL the slot still needed, after it held more than `max_slot_wal_keep_size`. The subscription it feeds cannot catch up, whatever it reports, and the rows it missed reach the other side only by a rebuild. A slot at a provider is the node's inbound side; a slot on the node is a subscriber's outbound side. The detector changed nothing.
gb-387-cause-1 = the node, or the site holding the slot, was cut off long enough for the other side to write more than its slot cap
gb-387-cause-2 = a subscription was disabled for a long time while its provider kept writing
gb-388-title = rows only the node holds have not been accepted
gb-388-what = Every site writes (ADR 1165), so a node being rebuilt may hold rows its founder lacks. The rebuild's export step compared each table of the founder's replication sets with the founder, by primary key and row digest, and wrote the rows only the node holds, or holds changed, to the export file beside the journal (mode 0600). Rows written here after the founder's last applied commit from the node are replayed after the copy. The others are for review: they came from another site, or the founder removed them since. The file also names each table that is not replicated, holds rows and references a table the copy truncates (on an identity database, the site's sessions), which the truncate empties. The step refuses until it is run with `--accept` and the digest it printed, so nothing is truncated before an operator has read them. The reseed step refuses with this code too when the rows only the node holds changed after the export: the export step then runs again. Nothing was changed.
gb-388-cause-1 = the node took writes while it was cut off, and the founder never received them
gb-388-cause-2 = the founder removed rows while the node was cut off, and the node still holds them
gb-388-cause-3 = a write reached the node after the export step (the reseed step's case)
gb-389-title = a client is connected to a database the rebuild truncates
gb-389-what = The rebuild's export and reseed steps read `pg_stat_activity` on the node, and a client other than the rebuild itself is connected to a database whose tables the rebuild truncates and copies again. A write that client makes after the export is in no file, and the truncate would lose it. The line names each database, role, application and client address. Nothing was changed.
gb-389-cause-1 = the site's services that write to these databases (the mesh management service, the identity services) are still running
gb-389-cause-2 = an exporter or an operator's psql session is connected
gb-390-title = the rebuild cannot read what it would truncate
gb-390-what = The rebuild refuses before it changes anything when it cannot tell what the copy would lose. Either the node subscribes to a site that is not one of the sides given, so the slot that site keeps for the node is not read; or the node has no subscription from its founder, so there is nothing to copy through; or a table has no primary key, so the rows only the node holds cannot be told apart. Nothing was changed.
gb-390-cause-1 = the node replicates with another joiner site that was not named with --peer
gb-390-cause-2 = the node's subscription from its founder was dropped by hand
gb-390-cause-3 = a table was added to a replication set without a primary key
gb-391-title = rows only the node held were not written back
gb-391-what = After the copy, the rebuild's replay step wrote each exported row to replay back on the node, one statement per row, parents before children, so it replicates to the founder. At least one row was not written (a key or a check refused it, or the row is now present with other contents). Each such row, with the reason, and the rows for review are in the hand-back file beside the journal (mode 0600). The step refuses until it is run with `--accept` and the hand-back's digest. The rows that were written stay written.
gb-391-cause-1 = another site wrote a row with the same unique value (an email, a name) while the node was cut off
gb-391-cause-2 = a row's parent was for review, not replayed, so its foreign key refuses it
gb-391-cause-3 = a trigger on the node refuses the write (a hub-only change made at a joiner)
gb-392-title = the secret store does not answer without a person
gb-392-what = A stand-up or a join-node reads and writes the secret store during the run (the site's secrets early, the service tokens later). Before it touches a host it asks the store for one read, bounded, with no terminal. The 1Password CLI did not answer in time, or refused: it is waiting for its prompt to be approved in the desktop app on this machine, the app is locked, or no session is signed in. An unattended run would have stopped at the first store call, minutes or an hour in, with nothing printed.
gb-392-cause-1 = the 1Password desktop app is locked, or its authorization for this terminal has lapsed
gb-392-cause-2 = no session: op is not signed in to the declared account
gb-392-cause-3 = the approval prompt is on a screen nobody is at
gb-312-title = a carrier rotation was asked for on a peering whose forwarder is declared by hand
gb-312-what = `goatbuilder federate rotate <partner> carrier` makes the edge's new carrier key in phase 2, on the edge. The forwarder must then hold that key: its entry for the peering, peering_forwarder_peerings[name=<partner>].edge_carrier_public_key, applied by peering-forwarder.yml. This peering's forwarder is declared by hand (peering_wg_via_forwarder: true in its edge group's group_vars; goatbuilder.yaml does not declare it), so that entry is in the operator's own group_vars, which the rotation cannot change, and its new value cannot be known before the rotation runs. The rotation would leave the carrier down, so it stopped before anything ran: no key was set aside and no rotation id was kept.
gb-312-cause-1 = the deployment declares its peering forwarder in hand-written group_vars rather than with forwarder: on the peering in goatbuilder.yaml
gb-317-title = a carrier rotation was asked for on a peering that has no carrier
gb-317-what = `goatbuilder federate rotate <partner> carrier` rotates the keys of the carrier, the link between a peering's edge and its peering forwarder. This peering declares no forwarder, neither with forwarder: on the peering in goatbuilder.yaml nor as peering_wg_via_forwarder: true in its edge group's group_vars, so its edge has no carrier and there is no carrier key to rotate. It stopped before anything ran: no key was set aside and no rotation id was kept.
gb-317-cause-1 = the peering's edge reaches the partner directly, with no peering forwarder
gb-317-cause-2 = the forwarder is declared in a group the run does not deploy to: the run reads --edge-group, else <partner>_edge, then peering_edges and all
